1
00:02:23,118 --> 00:02:25,110
Will!

2
00:02:25,662 --> 00:02:28,662
- Why is this happening?
- I don't know.

3
00:02:29,999 --> 00:02:32,121
You look beautiful.

4
00:02:33,627 --> 00:02:36,876
It's bad luck for the groom
to see the bride before the wedding.

5
00:02:37,006 --> 00:02:40,255
Make way! Let me through!
How dare you!

6
00:02:40,384 --> 00:02:43,835
Stand your men down at once.
Do you hear me?

7
00:02:45,263 --> 00:02:48,133
Governor Weatherby Swann,
it's been too long.

8
00:02:48,266 --> 00:02:51,800
- Cutler Beckett?
- It's Lord now, actually.

9
00:02:53,563 --> 00:02:58,805
Lord or not, you have no reason
and no authority to arrest this man.

10
00:02:59,276 --> 00:03:01,849
In fact, I do. Mr Mercer?

11
00:03:04,281 --> 00:03:07,815
The warrant for the arrest
of one William Turner.

12
00:03:12,873 --> 00:03:17,415
- This warrant is for Elizabeth Swann.
- Is it? That's annoying. My mistake.

13
00:03:17,544 --> 00:03:19,916
- Arrest her.
- On what charges?

14
00:03:20,046 --> 00:03:23,663
Aha. Here's the one for William Turner.

15
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:27,879
And I have another one
for a Mr James Norrington.

16
00:03:28,012 --> 00:03:30,134
- Is he present?
- What are the charges?

17
00:03:30,264 --> 00:03:33,928
Commodore Norrington resigned
his commission some months ago.

18
00:03:34,059 --> 00:03:36,431
That wasn't the answer
to the question I asked.

19
00:03:36,562 --> 00:03:40,179
Lord Beckett. In the category
of questions not answered...

20
00:03:40,315 --> 00:03:43,730
We are under the jurisdiction
of the king's governor of Port Royal

21
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:46,611
and you will tell us
what we are charged with.

22
00:03:46,738 --> 00:03:49,941
The charge is "conspiring to set free

23
00:03:50,074 --> 00:03:53,738
a man convicted of crimes
against the Crown and Empire

24
00:03:53,869 --> 00:03:57,617
and condemned to death,
for which the..."

25
00:03:57,748 --> 00:04:01,365
For which the punishment,
regrettably, is also death.

26
00:04:03,921 --> 00:04:07,005
Perhaps you remember a certain pirate
named Jack Sparrow.

27
00:04:07,132 --> 00:04:09,089
Captain.

28
00:04:10,385 --> 00:04:12,922
Captain Jack Sparrow.

29
00:04:13,179 --> 00:04:15,883
Captain Jack Sparrow.

30
00:04:16,015 --> 00:04:18,173
Yes, I thought you might.

31
00:04:20,019 --> 00:04:25,225
Fifteen men on a dead man's chest

32
00:04:25,357 --> 00:04:30,149
Yo-ho-ho and a bottle of rum

33
00:04:30,279 --> 00:04:35,569
Drink and the devil
had done for the rest

34
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:40,326
Yo-ho-ho and a bottle of rum

35
00:05:02,976 --> 00:05:05,098
Prisoners, come on!

36
00:06:43,195 --> 00:06:45,187
Sorry, mate.

37
00:06:47,366 --> 00:06:50,485
Mind if we make a little side trip?

38
00:06:51,370 --> 00:06:53,658
I didn't think so.

39
00:06:59,878 --> 00:07:03,745
- Not quite according to plan.
- Complications arose, ensued,

40
00:07:03,881 --> 00:07:05,873
were overcome.

41
00:07:07,343 --> 00:07:10,758
You got what you went in for, then?

42
00:07:13,140 --> 00:07:16,259
Captain, I think the crew,
meaning me as well,

43
00:07:16,393 --> 00:07:19,726
were expecting something
a bit more shiny,

44
00:07:19,855 --> 00:07:24,018
with the lsla de Muerta going pear-shaped,
reclaimed by the sea with the treasure.

45
00:07:24,150 --> 00:07:26,640
And the Royal Navy chasing us
around the Atlantic.

46
00:07:26,778 --> 00:07:29,447
- And the hurricane.
- Aye.

47
00:07:29,572 --> 00:07:35,277
All in all, it seems some time
since we did a speck of honest pirating.

48
00:07:36,537 --> 00:07:39,407
- Shiny.
- Aye, shiny.

49
00:07:40,582 --> 00:07:42,539
Is that how you're all feeling?

50
00:07:42,668 --> 00:07:48,006
That perhaps dear old Jack is not serving
your best interests as captain?

51
00:07:48,548 --> 00:07:50,374
Walk the plank.

52
00:07:50,508 --> 00:07:53,461
- What did the bird say?
- Do not blame the bird.

53
00:07:53,595 --> 00:07:56,928
Show us what is on
that piece of cloth there.

54
00:08:05,106 --> 00:08:08,640
- You know that don't do no good.
- It does me.

55
00:08:10,652 --> 00:08:13,937
- It's a key.
- No. Much more better.

56
00:08:15,157 --> 00:08:17,943
It is a drawing of a key.

57
00:08:24,374 --> 00:08:26,247
Gentlemen,

58
00:08:26,376 --> 00:08:28,249
what do keys do?

59
00:08:29,295 --> 00:08:31,121
Keys

60
00:08:31,255 --> 00:08:33,580
unlock things?

61
00:08:33,716 --> 00:08:37,499
And whatever this key unlocks,
inside there's something valuable.

62
00:08:37,636 --> 00:08:40,839
So we're setting out to find
whatever this key unlocks.

63
00:08:40,973 --> 00:08:42,550
No.

64
00:08:43,141 --> 00:08:47,008
If we don't have the key, we can't open
whatever we don't have that it unlocks.

65
00:08:47,145 --> 00:08:50,976
So what purpose would be served
in finding whatever need be unlocked,

66
00:08:51,107 --> 00:08:55,519
which we don't have, without first
having found the key what unlocks it?

67
00:08:55,653 --> 00:08:57,941
So we're going after this key.

68
00:08:58,906 --> 00:09:01,361
You're not making any sense at all.

69
00:09:01,909 --> 00:09:03,818
Any more questions?

70
00:09:03,952 --> 00:09:06,442
So do we have a heading?

71
00:09:07,706 --> 00:09:09,663
A heading.

72
00:09:11,001 --> 00:09:13,123
Set sail in a

73
00:09:14,212 --> 00:09:16,121
general

74
00:09:20,927 --> 00:09:22,753
that-way direction.

75
00:09:22,887 --> 00:09:24,001
Captain?

76
00:09:24,138 --> 00:09:28,846
Come on, snap to and make sail.
You know how this works. Go on.

77
00:09:32,396 --> 00:09:34,684
Have you noticed lately

78
00:09:34,815 --> 00:09:39,393
the captain seems to be acting
a bit strange... er?

79
00:09:39,528 --> 00:09:42,279
Setting sail without knowing
his own heading.

80
00:09:42,405 --> 00:09:45,358
Something's got Jack vexed,
and mark my words,

81
00:09:45,492 --> 00:09:49,323
what bodes ill for Jack Sparrow
bodes ill for us all.

82
00:10:03,634 --> 00:10:06,089
Lord Beckett,
the prisoner as ordered, sir.

83
00:10:06,219 --> 00:10:08,507
Those won't be necessary.

84
00:10:12,267 --> 00:10:17,059
The East lndia Trading Company
has need of your services.

85
00:10:21,108 --> 00:10:23,100
We wish for you to act as our agent

86
00:10:23,235 --> 00:10:27,314
in a business transaction
with our mutual friend Captain Sparrow.

87
00:10:27,448 --> 00:10:29,773
More acquaintance.
How do you know him?

88
00:10:29,908 --> 00:10:32,825
We've had dealings in the past.

89
00:10:33,745 --> 00:10:37,409
And we've each
left our mark on the other.

90
00:10:37,540 --> 00:10:39,865
What mark did he leave on you?

91
00:10:42,503 --> 00:10:46,286
By your efforts,
Jack Sparrow was set free.

92
00:10:46,424 --> 00:10:48,416
I would like you to go to him

93
00:10:48,551 --> 00:10:53,046
and recover a certain property
in his possession.

94
00:10:53,180 --> 00:10:55,884
Recover. At the point of a sword?

95
00:10:56,517 --> 00:10:58,474
Bargain.

96
00:11:04,774 --> 00:11:06,896
Letters of marque.

97
00:11:08,194 --> 00:11:10,731
You will offer
what amounts to a full pardon.

98
00:11:10,863 --> 00:11:15,074
Jack will be free,
a privateer in the employ of England.

99
00:11:15,826 --> 00:11:20,618
Somehow I doubt Jack will consider
employment the same as being free.

100
00:11:20,748 --> 00:11:22,787
Freedom.

101
00:11:23,751 --> 00:11:27,084
Jack Sparrow is a dying breed.

102
00:11:27,212 --> 00:11:29,038
The world is shrinking,

103
00:11:29,172 --> 00:11:32,540
the blank edges of the map filled in.

104
00:11:32,676 --> 00:11:36,507
Jack must find his place
in the New World or perish.

105
00:11:36,638 --> 00:11:39,389
Not unlike you, Mr Turner.

106
00:11:39,515 --> 00:11:42,301
You and your fiancee
face the hangman's noose.

107
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:47,768
- So you get Jack and the Black Pearl.
- The Black Pearl?

108
00:11:47,898 --> 00:11:52,061
- The property you want that he possesses.
- A ship? Hardly.

109
00:11:52,194 --> 00:11:56,238
The item in question is considerably
smaller and far more valuable,

110
00:11:56,364 --> 00:11:59,898
something Sparrow keeps
on his person at all times.

111
00:12:00,035 --> 00:12:02,608
A compass?

112
00:12:02,745 --> 00:12:04,867
You know it.

113
00:12:06,249 --> 00:12:10,578
Bring back that compass
or there's no deal.

114
00:12:36,027 --> 00:12:38,315
Why is the rum always gone?

115
00:12:46,912 --> 00:12:49,485
That's why.

116
00:12:55,503 --> 00:12:57,460
As you were, gents.

117
00:13:32,663 --> 00:13:34,536
Time's run out, Jack.

118
00:13:53,099 --> 00:13:55,672
Bootstrap.

119
00:13:55,810 --> 00:13:57,932
Bill Turner.

120
00:14:02,733 --> 00:14:05,058
You look good, Jack.

121
00:14:11,867 --> 00:14:14,025
Is this a dream?

122
00:14:14,161 --> 00:14:15,536
No.

123
00:14:15,662 --> 00:14:19,196
I thought not.
If it were, there'd be rum.

124
00:14:27,131 --> 00:14:29,586
You got the Pearl back, I see.

125
00:14:30,343 --> 00:14:34,126
I had some help retrieving the Pearl,
by the way.

126
00:14:35,306 --> 00:14:37,761
Your son.

127
00:14:37,891 --> 00:14:40,298
William?

128
00:14:41,103 --> 00:14:43,475
He ended up a pirate after all.

129
00:14:44,356 --> 00:14:48,270
And to what do I owe
the pleasure of your carbuncle?

130
00:14:48,401 --> 00:14:50,523
He sent me.

131
00:14:51,904 --> 00:14:54,525
Davy Jones.

132
00:14:57,326 --> 00:14:59,283
So it's you, then.

133
00:15:00,913 --> 00:15:04,198
He shanghaied you
into service, eh?

134
00:15:04,333 --> 00:15:06,290
I chose it.

135
00:15:06,418 --> 00:15:11,375
I'm sorry for the part I played
in the mutiny against you, Jack.

136
00:15:12,215 --> 00:15:14,705
I stood up for you.

137
00:15:14,843 --> 00:15:17,713
Everything went wrong after that.

138
00:15:19,430 --> 00:15:24,222
They strapped me to a cannon.
I ended up on the bottom of the ocean,

139
00:15:24,352 --> 00:15:27,222
the weight of the water
crushing down on me.

140
00:15:27,354 --> 00:15:29,844
Unable to move.

141
00:15:30,858 --> 00:15:33,183
Unable to die, Jack.

142
00:15:33,318 --> 00:15:38,857
And I thought that even the tiniest hope
of escaping this fate, I would take it.

143
00:15:39,491 --> 00:15:41,697
I would trade anything for it.

144
00:15:43,828 --> 00:15:46,994
It's funny what a man will do
to forestall his final judgment.

145
00:15:47,123 --> 00:15:49,744
You made a deal with him too, Jack.

146
00:15:49,876 --> 00:15:52,449
He raised the Pearl
from the depths for you.

147
00:15:52,586 --> 00:15:54,625
1 3 years, you've been her captain.

148
00:15:54,755 --> 00:15:56,628
- Technically...
- Jack.

149
00:15:56,757 --> 00:15:59,129
You won't be able
to talk yourself out of this.

150
00:15:59,259 --> 00:16:01,963
The terms what applied to me
apply to you as well.

151
00:16:02,095 --> 00:16:06,388
One soul bound to crew
1 00 years upon his ship.

152
00:16:06,516 --> 00:16:08,888
The Flying Dutchman
already has a captain...

153
00:16:09,019 --> 00:16:11,307
Then it's the locker for you!

154
00:16:11,438 --> 00:16:14,604
Jones's terrible leviathan will find you

155
00:16:14,732 --> 00:16:19,025
and drag the Pearl back to the depths
and you along with it.

156
00:16:19,987 --> 00:16:24,529
Any idea when Jones might release
said terrible beastie?

157
00:16:25,534 --> 00:16:27,407
I already told you, Jack.

158
00:16:29,413 --> 00:16:31,701
Your time is up.

159
00:16:32,624 --> 00:16:34,616
It comes now,

160
00:16:34,751 --> 00:16:40,041
drawn with ravenous hunger
to the man what bears the black spot.

161
00:16:49,223 --> 00:16:52,426
On deck, all hands!
Make fast the bunt gasket!

162
00:16:52,559 --> 00:16:54,800
On deck! Scurry!

163
00:16:54,937 --> 00:16:59,479
Scurry! I want movement!
Movement! I want movement!

164
00:16:59,608 --> 00:17:03,308
Lift the skin up! Keep your loof!
Haul those sheets!

165
00:17:03,445 --> 00:17:07,822
Run them! Run! Keep running! Run as if
the devil himself and itself is upon us!

166
00:17:07,949 --> 00:17:11,282
- Do we have a heading?
- Run! Land.

167
00:17:16,457 --> 00:17:20,121
- Which port?
- I didn't say "port", I said "land". Any land.

168
00:17:26,550 --> 00:17:29,716
- Jack's hat! Bring her about!
- No, no!

169
00:17:29,845 --> 00:17:32,086
Leave it!

170
00:17:32,764 --> 00:17:35,171
Run.

171
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:39,136
Back to your stations,
the lot of you!

172
00:17:47,736 --> 00:17:49,360
Jack?

173
00:17:49,488 --> 00:17:54,908
For the love of mother and child, Jack,
what's coming after us?

174
00:17:55,035 --> 00:17:56,742
Nothing.

175
00:19:10,147 --> 00:19:14,358
- Here, now, you can't be here.
- I think you'll find he can.

176
00:19:14,485 --> 00:19:15,895
Mr Swann!

177
00:19:16,028 --> 00:19:18,067
Governor Swann, still.

178
00:19:18,196 --> 00:19:21,399
Do you think I wear this wig
to keep my head warm?

179
00:19:21,533 --> 00:19:25,115
Jack's compass?
What does Beckett want with that?

180
00:19:25,245 --> 00:19:29,372
Does it matter? I'm to find Jack
and convince him to return to Port Royal.

181
00:19:29,499 --> 00:19:31,989
In exchange, the charges
against us will be dropped.

182
00:19:32,126 --> 00:19:35,743
No. We must find our own avenue
to secure your freedom.

183
00:19:35,880 --> 00:19:38,370
Is that a lack of faith in Jack
or in me?

184
00:19:38,507 --> 00:19:40,748
That you would risk your life
to save Sparrow

185
00:19:40,884 --> 00:19:44,751
does not mean
he would do the same for anyone else.

186
00:19:44,888 --> 00:19:48,256
Now, where is that dog with the keys?

187
00:19:49,809 --> 00:19:52,299
I have faith in you.

188
00:19:52,437 --> 00:19:55,010
Both of you.

189
00:19:56,566 --> 00:19:59,235
Where will you find him?

190
00:19:59,360 --> 00:20:04,068
Tortuga. I'll start there
and I won't stop searching till I find him.

191
00:20:04,532 --> 00:20:07,900
And then I intend to return here
to marry you.

192
00:20:08,035 --> 00:20:11,320
- Properly?
- Eagerly, if you'll still have me.

193
00:20:12,456 --> 00:20:16,453
If it weren't for these bars,
I'd have you already.

194
00:20:22,966 --> 00:20:27,461
- I'll wait for you.
- Keep a weather eye on the horizon.

195
00:20:36,979 --> 00:20:39,101
Captain Jack Sparrow?

196
00:20:40,190 --> 00:20:44,519
Owes me four doubloons.
Heard he was dead.

197
00:20:44,653 --> 00:20:48,436
Singapore. That's what I heard.
Drunk, with a smile on his face.

198
00:20:48,573 --> 00:20:52,902
Sure as the tide,
Jack Sparrow turn up in Singapore.

199
00:20:53,035 --> 00:20:54,992
Jack Sparrow?

200
00:20:55,121 --> 00:20:57,160
I haven't seen him in a month.

201
00:20:57,289 --> 00:21:01,037
When you find him,
will you give him a message?

202
00:21:02,127 --> 00:21:04,617
Can't say about Jack Sparrow,

203
00:21:04,755 --> 00:21:07,376
but there's an island
just south of the straits

204
00:21:07,507 --> 00:21:10,293
where I trade spice for

205
00:21:10,427 --> 00:21:12,502
delicious long pork.

206
00:21:14,180 --> 00:21:18,224
Cannot say about Jack,
but you'll find a ship there.

207
00:21:18,351 --> 00:21:20,639
A ship with black sails.

208
00:21:28,152 --> 00:21:30,025
My brother will take you ashore.

209
00:21:40,872 --> 00:21:43,789
- What's wrong? The beach is there.
- C'est trop dangereux.

210
00:21:43,917 --> 00:21:47,332
- What?
- C'est trop dangereux. je vous l'avais dit.

211
00:21:48,838 --> 00:21:50,830
Bon voyage. monsieur.

212
00:22:15,863 --> 00:22:18,318
Jack!

213
00:22:18,449 --> 00:22:20,406
Jack Sparrow!

214
00:22:26,373 --> 00:22:28,330
Marty!

215
00:22:29,209 --> 00:22:30,916
Cotton!

216
00:22:31,920 --> 00:22:33,877
Anybody?

217
00:22:40,220 --> 00:22:41,797
A familiar face.

218
00:22:41,929 --> 00:22:43,672
Don't eat me.

219
00:22:43,806 --> 00:22:46,759
- I'm not going to eat you.
- Don't eat me.

220
00:22:46,892 --> 00:22:49,429
No. Don't eat me.

221
00:23:00,405 --> 00:23:02,362
Gibbs.

222
00:23:32,644 --> 00:23:34,636
Come on! Let's go!

223
00:23:37,732 --> 00:23:39,558
Come on! Who wants it?

224
00:23:42,445 --> 00:23:44,733
I can do this all day!

225
00:24:49,132 --> 00:24:50,626
Jack?

226
00:24:50,759 --> 00:24:53,131
Jack Sparrow!

227
00:24:53,261 --> 00:24:55,798
I can honestly say
I'm glad to see you.

228
00:25:04,605 --> 00:25:06,514
Jack, it's me.

229
00:25:06,649 --> 00:25:07,929
Will Turner.

230
00:25:14,573 --> 00:25:16,861
Tell them to let me down!

231
00:25:19,035 --> 00:25:20,992
..eensy-weensy.

232
00:25:27,085 --> 00:25:29,207
..eunuchy. Snip, snip.

233
00:25:31,047 --> 00:25:33,253
Eunuchy.

234
00:25:36,135 --> 00:25:38,542
Jack, the compass.
That's all I need.

235
00:25:38,679 --> 00:25:41,715
Elizabeth is in danger.
We were arrested for helping you.

236
00:25:41,849 --> 00:25:44,137
She faces the gallows!

237
00:25:51,816 --> 00:25:53,559
Savvy?

238
00:25:59,907 --> 00:26:02,279
Save me.

239
00:26:02,410 --> 00:26:04,449
Jack, what did you tell them?

240
00:26:04,578 --> 00:26:06,570
No!

241
00:26:07,039 --> 00:26:09,280
What about Elizabeth?

242
00:26:09,416 --> 00:26:10,958
Jack!

243
00:26:13,503 --> 00:26:16,040
That's a good girl.
Come a little closer.

244
00:26:16,172 --> 00:26:20,501
- We don't bite.
- Go on. We won't bite you.

245
00:26:23,054 --> 00:26:25,591
Come quickly.

246
00:26:25,723 --> 00:26:28,972
- Tell me what's happening.
- Our name still has some standing.

247
00:26:29,101 --> 00:26:32,386
I've arranged passage to England.
The captain is a friend of mine.

248
00:26:32,521 --> 00:26:34,643
No! Will's gone to find Jack!

249
00:26:34,773 --> 00:26:36,765
We cannot count on William Turner.

250
00:26:36,900 --> 00:26:40,019
- Come!
- He's better than you give him credit for.

251
00:26:40,153 --> 00:26:43,521
This is no time for innocence.
Beckett has offered one pardon only -

252
00:26:43,657 --> 00:26:45,945
one, and that is promised
to Jack Sparrow.

253
00:26:46,076 --> 00:26:47,735
Even if Will succeeds,

254
00:26:47,869 --> 00:26:53,906
do not ask me to endure the sight of my
daughter walking to the gallows. Do not.

255
00:26:54,041 --> 00:26:58,418
Perhaps I can ensure a fair trial
for Will if he returns.

256
00:26:58,546 --> 00:27:03,587
- A fair trial for Will ends in a hanging.
- Then there is nothing left for you here.

257
00:27:10,765 --> 00:27:12,887
Wait inside.

258
00:27:14,018 --> 00:27:15,975
Captain?

259
00:27:18,523 --> 00:27:20,480
Captain.

260
00:27:26,322 --> 00:27:28,610
Evening, Governor.

261
00:27:28,741 --> 00:27:30,698
Shame, that.

262
00:27:30,826 --> 00:27:32,901
He was carrying this.

263
00:27:33,036 --> 00:27:35,705
It's a letter to the king.

264
00:27:35,831 --> 00:27:38,119
- It's from you.
- No...

265
00:27:41,544 --> 00:27:43,702
Elizabeth.

266
00:27:44,464 --> 00:27:46,789
What are you doing?

267
00:27:49,552 --> 00:27:52,007
- Where is she?
- Who?

268
00:28:09,779 --> 00:28:13,942
No doubt you've discovered that loyalty
is no longer the currency of the realm,

269
00:28:14,075 --> 00:28:16,316
as your father believes.

270
00:28:17,161 --> 00:28:19,782
Then what is?

271
00:28:19,914 --> 00:28:23,117
I'm afraid currency
is the currency of the realm.

272
00:28:23,250 --> 00:28:28,492
I expect, then, we can come
to some sort of understanding.

273
00:28:28,630 --> 00:28:31,796
- I'm here to negotiate.
- I'm listening.

274
00:28:34,386 --> 00:28:36,627
I'm listening intently.

275
00:28:38,556 --> 00:28:40,928
These letters of marque
are signed by the king.

276
00:28:41,059 --> 00:28:45,767
Yes, and they're not valid
until they bear my signature and my seal.

277
00:28:45,896 --> 00:28:48,565
Or else I would not still be here.

278
00:28:48,691 --> 00:28:52,688
You sent Will to get you the compass
owned by Jack Sparrow.

279
00:28:52,820 --> 00:28:56,484
- It will do you no good.
- Do explain.

280
00:28:56,615 --> 00:29:01,360
I have been to the lsla de Muerta.
I have seen the treasure myself.

281
00:29:01,494 --> 00:29:05,705
- There is something you need to know.
- I see.

282
00:29:05,832 --> 00:29:08,916
You think the compass
leads only to the lsla de Muerta

283
00:29:09,043 --> 00:29:12,162
and so you hope to save me
from an evil fate.

284
00:29:12,922 --> 00:29:15,329
But you mustn't worry.

285
00:29:15,925 --> 00:29:19,258
I care not for cursed Aztec gold.

286
00:29:19,386 --> 00:29:21,841
My desires are not so provincial.

287
00:29:22,598 --> 00:29:26,844
There's more than one chest of value
in these waters.

288
00:29:28,144 --> 00:29:32,141
So perhaps you may wish
to enhance your offer.

289
00:29:34,651 --> 00:29:39,478
Consider into your calculations
that you robbed me of my wedding night.

290
00:29:39,614 --> 00:29:41,736
So I did.

291
00:29:43,701 --> 00:29:45,989
A marriage interrupted

292
00:29:48,330 --> 00:29:50,999
or fate intervenes?

293
00:29:51,750 --> 00:29:54,999
You're making great efforts
to ensure Jack Sparrow's freedom.

294
00:29:55,128 --> 00:29:57,618
- These aren't going to Jack.
- Really?

295
00:29:57,756 --> 00:30:00,460
To ensure Mr Turner's freedom, then,

296
00:30:01,384 --> 00:30:04,088
I'll still want that compass.

297
00:30:04,220 --> 00:30:07,802
Consider that in your calculations.

298
00:30:27,450 --> 00:30:30,486
I say it was Divine Providence
what escaped us from jail.

299
00:30:30,620 --> 00:30:33,289
And I say it was me being clever.

300
00:30:34,582 --> 00:30:37,119
Ain't that right, poochie?

301
00:30:37,251 --> 00:30:41,378
Well, how'd you know it weren't Divine
Providence what inspired you to be clever?

302
00:30:41,505 --> 00:30:43,627
Anyways, I ain't stealing no ship.

303
00:30:43,757 --> 00:30:48,999
It ain't stealing. It's salvaging.
And since when did you care?

304
00:30:49,137 --> 00:30:53,715
Since we're not immortal no more.
We gotta take care of our immortal souls.

305
00:30:53,850 --> 00:30:57,598
- You know you can't read.
- It's the Bible. You get credit for trying.

306
00:30:57,729 --> 00:31:03,185
Pretending to read the Bible's a lie.
That's a mark against...

307
00:31:05,069 --> 00:31:06,728
Look.

308
00:31:06,862 --> 00:31:08,901
There it is.

309
00:31:13,619 --> 00:31:15,611
What's got into him?

310
00:31:15,746 --> 00:31:17,821
Must have seen a catfish.

311
00:31:25,046 --> 00:31:27,168
Stupid mongrel!

312
00:31:43,063 --> 00:31:45,020
Come on!

313
00:31:52,155 --> 00:31:56,366
- It's ours for the taking.
- Tide's coming in. That should help.

314
00:31:56,492 --> 00:31:59,243
And salvaging is saving,
in a manner of speaking.

315
00:31:59,370 --> 00:32:01,196
There's the truth of it!

316
00:32:08,795 --> 00:32:11,748
Suppose we better save it
as soon as we can,

317
00:32:11,882 --> 00:32:15,048
what with our souls
in such a vulnerable state and all.

318
00:32:15,176 --> 00:32:17,797
Amen to that.

319
00:32:34,027 --> 00:32:36,019
Thank you.

320
00:32:52,920 --> 00:32:56,169
Why would he do this to us?
If Jack is the chief...

321
00:32:56,298 --> 00:32:58,705
Aye, the Pelegostos
made Jack their chief,

322
00:32:58,842 --> 00:33:02,376
but he only remains chief
as long as he acts like a chief.

323
00:33:02,513 --> 00:33:05,597
So he had no choice.
He's a captive as much as the rest of us.

324
00:33:05,724 --> 00:33:12,390
Worse, as it turns out. See, the Pelegostos
believe that Jack is a god in human form,

325
00:33:12,522 --> 00:33:18,441
and they intend to do him the honour
of releasing him from his fleshy prison.

326
00:33:22,156 --> 00:33:24,563
They'll roast him and eat him.

327
00:33:25,659 --> 00:33:27,402
Where's the rest of the crew?

328
00:33:27,536 --> 00:33:32,078
These cages we're in
weren't built till after we got here.

329
00:33:34,418 --> 00:33:36,576
The feast is about to begin.

330
00:33:37,212 --> 00:33:39,370
Jack's life will end

331
00:33:39,506 --> 00:33:41,794
when the drums stop.

332
00:33:43,551 --> 00:33:46,124
Well, we can't just sit here and wait,
then, can we?

333
00:33:48,598 --> 00:33:51,219
No, no. Oi! No, no.

334
00:33:51,350 --> 00:33:54,434
More wood. Big fire. Big fire.

335
00:33:54,562 --> 00:33:56,637
I am Chief. Want big fire.

336
00:33:56,772 --> 00:33:58,930
Come on.

337
00:34:01,985 --> 00:34:04,606
Tout de suite.
Come on. More wood.

338
00:35:13,260 --> 00:35:15,217
Bugger.

339
00:35:24,938 --> 00:35:27,060
A little seasoning, eh?

340
00:35:40,911 --> 00:35:42,488
Well done.

341
00:36:21,867 --> 00:36:25,200
Put your legs through.
Start to climb!

342
00:36:26,287 --> 00:36:30,414
Come on, men!
It'll take all of us to crew the Black Pearl!

343
00:36:30,541 --> 00:36:33,245
Actually, you wouldn't need everyone.

344
00:36:33,377 --> 00:36:35,665
About six would do.

345
00:36:39,300 --> 00:36:41,043
Oh, dear.

346
00:36:44,555 --> 00:36:47,176
- Hurry!
- Come on!

347
00:36:50,977 --> 00:36:53,349
Come on! Is that all you got?

348
00:37:01,028 --> 00:37:03,518
Wait. Stop.

349
00:37:04,073 --> 00:37:06,030
Stop.

350
00:37:18,461 --> 00:37:20,833
- What are they doing?
- Stop.

351
00:37:27,595 --> 00:37:29,302
Snake!

352
00:37:46,780 --> 00:37:48,571
Move!

353
00:38:14,806 --> 00:38:16,964
Well, go on, go get them.

354
00:38:22,563 --> 00:38:24,769
No, no. Oi! No, no!

355
00:38:27,151 --> 00:38:28,775
Not good.

356
00:38:45,084 --> 00:38:47,290
Cut it loose! Find a rock!

357
00:39:06,313 --> 00:39:08,056
Roll the cage!

358
00:39:08,190 --> 00:39:10,515
Come on!

359
00:39:35,090 --> 00:39:40,000
- Lift the cage! Hurry!
- Come on, lads. Lift it like a lady's skirt.

360
00:40:36,231 --> 00:40:37,559
Run!

361
00:40:51,328 --> 00:40:54,032
This way, lads!

362
00:41:16,602 --> 00:41:18,096
Take cover!

363
00:41:19,313 --> 00:41:20,807
Stop it!

364
00:42:15,908 --> 00:42:17,318
Bugger.

365
00:42:44,935 --> 00:42:47,556
Haul loose the mooring line!

366
00:42:47,687 --> 00:42:49,893
The mooring line!

367
00:42:50,023 --> 00:42:53,640
Thief! Little hairy thief!
Give it back!

368
00:42:53,777 --> 00:42:57,062
- Don't bite it!
- Haul loose the mooring line!

369
00:42:57,613 --> 00:42:59,688
He's got me eye.
He won't give it back.

370
00:42:59,824 --> 00:43:03,239
- How'd you get it back last time?
- Excellent! Our work's half done.

371
00:43:03,369 --> 00:43:05,824
We done it for you,
knowing you'd be coming back.

372
00:43:05,955 --> 00:43:08,825
- Make ready to sail, boys!
- What about Jack?

373
00:43:08,957 --> 00:43:11,245
I won't leave without him.

374
00:43:20,427 --> 00:43:23,380
- Time to go.
- Cast off those lines!

375
00:43:32,104 --> 00:43:33,930
Make ready to cast off!

376
00:43:38,735 --> 00:43:41,060
Good doggy.

377
00:43:56,502 --> 00:44:02,789
Alas, my children, this is the day you shall
always remember as the day you almost...

378
00:44:05,928 --> 00:44:08,086
Captain Jack Sparrow.

379
00:44:33,912 --> 00:44:36,948
Let's get away from this island
and head out to open sea.

380
00:44:37,082 --> 00:44:41,542
Yes to the first. Yes to the second, but
only insofar as we keep to the shallows.

381
00:44:41,670 --> 00:44:43,745
That seems a bit contradictory, Captain.

382
00:44:43,880 --> 00:44:46,999
I have faith in your reconciliatory
navigational skills.

383
00:44:47,133 --> 00:44:50,169
Now, where is that monkey?
I want to shoot something.

384
00:44:55,891 --> 00:44:58,216
Jack. Elizabeth is in danger.

385
00:44:58,352 --> 00:45:01,103
Have you considered
just locking her up somewhere?

386
00:45:01,230 --> 00:45:03,720
She is locked up,
bound to hang for helping you.

387
00:45:03,857 --> 00:45:08,814
There comes a time when one must
take responsibility for one's mistakes.

388
00:45:10,113 --> 00:45:12,485
I need that compass of yours, Jack.

389
00:45:12,615 --> 00:45:15,152
I must trade it for her freedom.

390
00:45:17,495 --> 00:45:20,116
- Mr Gibbs.
- Captain.

391
00:45:20,247 --> 00:45:23,247
We have a need to travel upriver.

392
00:45:23,375 --> 00:45:26,826
By need, do you mean a trifling need?

393
00:45:26,962 --> 00:45:30,710
Fleeting?
As in, say, a passing fancy?

394
00:45:30,841 --> 00:45:34,589
No, a resolute and unyielding need.

395
00:45:34,719 --> 00:45:38,467
What we need to do
is make sail for Port Royal with all haste.

396
00:45:38,598 --> 00:45:40,471
William,

397
00:45:40,600 --> 00:45:42,592
I shall trade you the compass

398
00:45:42,727 --> 00:45:44,518
if you will help me

399
00:45:44,645 --> 00:45:46,767
to find this.

400
00:45:50,484 --> 00:45:52,939
- You want me to find this?
- No.

401
00:45:54,113 --> 00:45:56,520
You want you to find this,

402
00:45:56,657 --> 00:46:00,701
because the finding of this
finds you incapacitorially finding

403
00:46:00,827 --> 00:46:07,493
and/or locating in your discovering a way
to save your dolly belle, ol' what's-her-face.

404
00:46:07,625 --> 00:46:10,411
Savvy?

405
00:46:12,004 --> 00:46:14,494
This is going to save Elizabeth?

406
00:46:16,842 --> 00:46:19,878
How much do you know
about Davy Jones?

407
00:46:22,139 --> 00:46:23,681
Not much.

408
00:46:23,807 --> 00:46:26,760
Yeah, it's going to save Elizabeth.

409
00:46:53,126 --> 00:46:55,747
What's all this?

410
00:46:56,338 --> 00:47:01,332
If you both fancy the dress, you'll just have
to share and wear it one after the other.

411
00:47:01,468 --> 00:47:04,753
It's not like that, sir.
This ship is haunted.

412
00:47:05,346 --> 00:47:07,587
Is it, now? And you?

413
00:47:07,723 --> 00:47:11,802
There's a female presence
amongst us here, sir.

414
00:47:11,936 --> 00:47:14,391
All the men, they can feel it.

415
00:47:14,522 --> 00:47:18,222
It's the ghost of a lady widowed
before her marriage, I figure it,

416
00:47:18,358 --> 00:47:20,599
searching for her husband,
lost at sea.

417
00:47:20,736 --> 00:47:23,273
A virgin too, likely as not.

418
00:47:23,405 --> 00:47:25,611
And that bodes ill by all accounts.

419
00:47:25,740 --> 00:47:30,200
I say that we throw the dress overboard
and we hope the spirit follows it.

420
00:47:30,328 --> 00:47:33,114
No! That will just anger the spirit, sir.

421
00:47:33,247 --> 00:47:37,244
What we need to do is find out what
the spirit needs, and then get it back to her.

422
00:47:37,376 --> 00:47:39,582
Enough! Enough!

423
00:47:39,712 --> 00:47:43,958
You're a pair of superstitious goats
and it's got the best of you.

424
00:47:44,091 --> 00:47:47,257
Now, this appears to be no more
as we have a stowaway on board.

425
00:47:47,386 --> 00:47:49,508
A young woman, by the look of it.

426
00:47:49,638 --> 00:47:52,211
I want you to search the ship
and find her.

427
00:47:52,349 --> 00:47:55,053
Oh, and she's probably naked.

428
00:48:25,004 --> 00:48:27,577
Why is Jack afraid of the open ocean?

429
00:48:27,715 --> 00:48:30,040
Well, if you believe such things,

430
00:48:30,593 --> 00:48:33,344
there's a beast does the bidding
of Davy Jones -

431
00:48:33,471 --> 00:48:38,013
a fearsome creature with giant tentacles
that'll suction your face clean off

432
00:48:38,142 --> 00:48:41,724
and drag an entire ship
down to the crushing darkness.

433
00:48:43,397 --> 00:48:45,519
The kraken.

434
00:48:47,818 --> 00:48:51,685
They say the stench of its breath is like...

435
00:48:51,821 --> 00:48:55,272
Imagine, the last thing you know
on God's green earth

436
00:48:55,408 --> 00:49:01,327
is the roar of the kraken and the reeking
odour of a thousand rotting corpses.

437
00:49:03,249 --> 00:49:05,704
If you believe such things.

438
00:49:06,252 --> 00:49:08,624
And the key will spare him that?

439
00:49:08,754 --> 00:49:12,205
Well, that's the very question
Jack wants answered.

440
00:49:12,341 --> 00:49:14,796
Bad enough even to go visit

441
00:49:15,760 --> 00:49:17,882
her.

442
00:49:18,013 --> 00:49:19,839
Her?

443
00:49:19,973 --> 00:49:21,930
Aye.

444
00:49:56,966 --> 00:50:00,630
No worries, mates.
Tia Dalma and I go way back.

445
00:50:00,761 --> 00:50:04,675
Thick as thieves.
Nigh inseparable, we are.

446
00:50:04,807 --> 00:50:07,095
Were. Have been.

447
00:50:08,310 --> 00:50:09,305
Before.

448
00:50:09,436 --> 00:50:12,353
- I'll watch your back.
- It's me front I'm worried about.

449
00:50:12,480 --> 00:50:14,935
- Mind the boat.
- Mind the boat.

450
00:50:15,817 --> 00:50:18,272
- Mind the boat.
- Mind the boat.

451
00:50:18,403 --> 00:50:19,683
Mind the boat.

452
00:50:20,655 --> 00:50:22,777
Mind the boat.

453
00:50:34,459 --> 00:50:37,708
Jack Sparrow.

454
00:50:37,838 --> 00:50:39,913
Tia Dalma.

455
00:50:40,757 --> 00:50:45,299
I always knew the wind was going
to blow you back to me one day.

456
00:50:50,016 --> 00:50:51,925
You...

457
00:50:54,395 --> 00:50:56,968
You have a touch of

458
00:50:57,106 --> 00:51:00,439
destiny about you,

459
00:51:00,567 --> 00:51:02,393
William Turner.

460
00:51:04,196 --> 00:51:06,318
You know me?

461
00:51:06,448 --> 00:51:09,021
You want to know me.

462
00:51:09,159 --> 00:51:13,737
There'll be no knowing here. We've come
for help and we're not leaving without it.

463
00:51:13,871 --> 00:51:17,488
- I thought I knew you.
- Not so well as I had hoped.

464
00:51:17,625 --> 00:51:19,747
- Come.
- Come.

465
00:51:23,505 --> 00:51:27,965
What service may I do you?

466
00:51:28,719 --> 00:51:32,633
- You know I demand payment.
- I brought payment.

467
00:51:36,267 --> 00:51:38,224
Look.

468
00:51:39,437 --> 00:51:41,809
An undead monkey.

469
00:51:41,939 --> 00:51:44,061
Top that.

470
00:51:48,029 --> 00:51:51,896
Don't! You've no idea
how long it took us to catch that.

471
00:51:52,408 --> 00:51:54,863
The payment is fair.

472
00:51:54,993 --> 00:51:56,950
We're looking for this.

473
00:51:59,539 --> 00:52:02,325
And what it goes to.

474
00:52:03,835 --> 00:52:08,461
The compass you bartered from me,
it cannot lead you to this?

475
00:52:08,590 --> 00:52:12,005
Maybe. Why?

476
00:52:14,512 --> 00:52:18,924
Jack Sparrow does not know
what he wants.

477
00:52:19,600 --> 00:52:21,841
Or do you know,

478
00:52:21,977 --> 00:52:25,844
but are loath to claim it as your own?

479
00:52:27,607 --> 00:52:29,599
Your key go to a chest.

480
00:52:30,235 --> 00:52:34,730
And it is what lay inside the chest
you seek, don't it?

481
00:52:34,864 --> 00:52:37,734
- What is inside?
- Gold?

482
00:52:37,867 --> 00:52:41,911
Jewels? Unclaimed properties
of a valuable nature?

483
00:52:42,038 --> 00:52:45,157
Nothing bad, I hope?

484
00:52:45,958 --> 00:52:48,792
You know of Davy Jones,

485
00:52:48,919 --> 00:52:50,413
yes?

486
00:52:50,546 --> 00:52:53,297
A man of the sea.

487
00:52:53,423 --> 00:52:55,664
A great sailor,

488
00:52:55,801 --> 00:53:00,758
until he run afoul of that
which vex all men.

489
00:53:00,889 --> 00:53:03,842
What vexes all men?

490
00:53:05,435 --> 00:53:07,890
What indeed?

491
00:53:08,479 --> 00:53:10,436
- The sea.
- Sums.

492
00:53:10,564 --> 00:53:13,517
The dichotomy of good and evil.

493
00:53:14,193 --> 00:53:15,852
A woman.

494
00:53:16,195 --> 00:53:17,772
A woman.

495
00:53:17,905 --> 00:53:19,944
He fell in love.

496
00:53:20,073 --> 00:53:22,990
No, no, no, no.
I heard it was the sea he fell in love with.

497
00:53:23,118 --> 00:53:27,032
Same story, different versions,
and all are true.

498
00:53:27,163 --> 00:53:29,488
See, it was a woman

499
00:53:29,624 --> 00:53:32,328
as changing and harsh

500
00:53:32,460 --> 00:53:35,828
and untamable as the sea.

501
00:53:35,963 --> 00:53:38,335
Him never stopped loving her.

502
00:53:38,466 --> 00:53:43,211
But the pain it cause him
was too much to live with

503
00:53:43,345 --> 00:53:46,014
but not enough to cause him to die.

504
00:53:47,808 --> 00:53:52,220
What exactly did he put
into the chest?

505
00:53:52,354 --> 00:53:54,263
Him heart.

506
00:53:54,397 --> 00:53:56,436
Literally or figuratively?

507
00:53:56,566 --> 00:54:01,061
He couldn't literally
put his heart in a chest.

508
00:54:01,195 --> 00:54:03,152
Could he?

509
00:54:03,281 --> 00:54:09,318
It was not worth feeling
what small, fleeting joy life brings.

510
00:54:09,453 --> 00:54:11,077
And so

511
00:54:11,205 --> 00:54:13,874
him carve out him heart,

512
00:54:14,500 --> 00:54:19,161
Iock it away in a chest
and hide the chest from the world.

513
00:54:20,088 --> 00:54:22,127
The key

514
00:54:22,257 --> 00:54:26,752
he keep with him at all times.

515
00:54:27,428 --> 00:54:31,092
- You knew this.
- I did not. I didn't know where the key was.

516
00:54:31,224 --> 00:54:35,387
But now we do. All that's left is to climb
aboard the Flying Dutchman, grab the key,

517
00:54:35,519 --> 00:54:38,223
you go back to Port Royal
and save your bonnie lass, eh?

518
00:54:38,355 --> 00:54:41,189
Let me see your hand.

519
00:54:54,787 --> 00:54:57,242
The black spot!

520
00:54:58,332 --> 00:55:01,831
- The black spot!
- Black spot!

521
00:55:02,294 --> 00:55:05,330
My eyesight's as good as ever,
just so you know.

522
00:55:05,464 --> 00:55:08,915
I have just the thing.
Now, where did I put it?

523
00:55:12,387 --> 00:55:15,838
My little beauty, where are you?

524
00:55:15,974 --> 00:55:19,473
Such a long time in such a mess.

525
00:55:21,563 --> 00:55:24,397
Davy Jones cannot make port.

526
00:55:24,524 --> 00:55:27,809
Cannot step on land
but once every ten years.

527
00:55:28,402 --> 00:55:31,605
Land is where you are safe,
Jack Sparrow,

528
00:55:31,739 --> 00:55:34,229
and so you will carry land with you.

529
00:55:43,542 --> 00:55:45,499
Dirt.

530
00:55:46,669 --> 00:55:50,286
- This is a jar of dirt.
- Yes.

531
00:55:52,675 --> 00:55:55,509
Is the jar of dirt going to help?

532
00:55:55,636 --> 00:55:58,506
If you don't want it, give it back.

533
00:55:59,056 --> 00:56:02,175
- No.
- Then it helps.

534
00:56:04,436 --> 00:56:09,430
It seems we have a need to find
the Flying Dutchman.

535
00:56:12,610 --> 00:56:16,393
A touch of destiny.

536
00:56:31,920 --> 00:56:34,410
That's the Flying Dutchman?

537
00:56:36,383 --> 00:56:39,134
- She doesn't look like much.
- Neither do you.

538
00:56:39,261 --> 00:56:41,549
Do not underestimate her.

539
00:56:43,056 --> 00:56:47,433
- Must have run afoul of the reef.
- So, what's your plan, then?

540
00:56:47,560 --> 00:56:50,975
I row over, search the ship
until I find your bloody key.

541
00:56:51,105 --> 00:56:53,430
And if there are crewmen?

542
00:56:53,566 --> 00:56:55,772
I cut down anyone in my path.

543
00:56:56,652 --> 00:56:59,522
I like it. Simple, easy to remember.

544
00:56:59,655 --> 00:57:02,608
Your chariot awaits you, Sire.

545
00:57:07,162 --> 00:57:12,867
If you do happen to get captured, just say
Jack Sparrow sent you to settle his debt.

546
00:57:13,001 --> 00:57:16,868
- It might save your life.
- Bon voyage!

547
00:57:20,883 --> 00:57:23,420
Douse the lamps.

548
00:58:05,133 --> 00:58:06,164
Sailor.

549
00:58:06,301 --> 00:58:08,838
- Hoisting the jib, Captain's orders.
- Sailor!

550
00:58:08,970 --> 00:58:11,295
Hoisting the jib.
Bring up with a round turn.

551
00:58:11,430 --> 00:58:14,099
There's no use.
You've run aground.

552
00:58:14,225 --> 00:58:15,553
No.

553
00:58:15,684 --> 00:58:17,806
Beneath us.

554
00:58:18,687 --> 00:58:21,059
Foul breath.

555
00:59:13,656 --> 00:59:16,490
Down on your marrowbones and pray.

556
00:59:31,422 --> 00:59:35,336
Get back! Get back!

557
01:00:06,956 --> 01:00:10,573
Five men still alive.
The rest have moved on.

558
01:00:27,767 --> 01:00:30,886
Do you fear death?

559
01:00:32,355 --> 01:00:37,147
Do you fear that dark abyss?

560
01:00:39,069 --> 01:00:41,855
All your deeds laid bare.

561
01:00:42,781 --> 01:00:45,734
All your sins punished.

562
01:00:47,619 --> 01:00:50,074
I can offer you

563
01:00:50,205 --> 01:00:52,612
an escape.

564
01:00:52,749 --> 01:00:55,785
Don't listen to him!

565
01:01:04,468 --> 01:01:07,421
Do you not fear death?

566
01:01:09,932 --> 01:01:12,469
I'll take my chances, sir.

567
01:01:12,601 --> 01:01:14,973
To the depths.

568
01:01:20,483 --> 01:01:22,475
Cruel blackguard!

569
01:01:22,610 --> 01:01:24,732
Life is cruel.

570
01:01:25,696 --> 01:01:28,447
Why should the afterlife
be any different?

571
01:01:28,991 --> 01:01:32,110
I offer you a choice.

572
01:01:32,244 --> 01:01:34,913
Join my crew

573
01:01:35,038 --> 01:01:37,326
and postpone the judgment.

574
01:01:38,792 --> 01:01:42,243
One hundred years before the mast.

575
01:01:44,589 --> 01:01:46,498
Will ye serve?

576
01:01:48,343 --> 01:01:50,300
I will serve.

577
01:01:59,728 --> 01:02:03,179
You are neither dead nor dying.

578
01:02:03,315 --> 01:02:05,888
What is your purpose here?

579
01:02:08,737 --> 01:02:11,856
Jack Sparrow sent me
to settle his debt.

580
01:02:13,033 --> 01:02:16,236
What is your purpose here?

581
01:02:16,369 --> 01:02:19,535
Jack Sparrow
sent me to settle his debt.

582
01:02:22,541 --> 01:02:25,078
Did he, now?

583
01:02:25,211 --> 01:02:28,626
I'm sorely tempted to accept that offer.

584
01:02:45,563 --> 01:02:48,349
You have a debt to pay.

585
01:02:48,482 --> 01:02:51,850
You've been captain of the Black Pearl
for 1 3 years.

586
01:02:51,986 --> 01:02:54,025
That was our agreement.

587
01:02:54,154 --> 01:02:57,937
Technically I was only captain
for two years, then I was mutinied upon.

588
01:02:58,075 --> 01:03:01,823
Then you were a poor captain,
but a captain nonetheless.

589
01:03:01,953 --> 01:03:05,156
Have you not introduced yourself
all these years

590
01:03:05,290 --> 01:03:07,863
as Captain Jack Sparrow?

591
01:03:10,837 --> 01:03:13,754
You have my payment.
One soul to serve on your ship.

592
01:03:13,881 --> 01:03:16,371
One soul is not equal to another.

593
01:03:17,218 --> 01:03:23,718
We've established my proposal is sound
in principle. Now we're haggling over price.

594
01:03:23,849 --> 01:03:26,386
Price?

595
01:03:28,437 --> 01:03:31,888
Just how many souls
do you think my soul is worth?

596
01:03:35,694 --> 01:03:38,315
One hundred souls.

597
01:03:39,280 --> 01:03:41,023
Three days.

598
01:03:41,574 --> 01:03:45,405
You're a diamond, mate. Send me back
the boy. I'll get started right off.

599
01:03:45,536 --> 01:03:48,620
I keep the boy.
A good-faith payment.

600
01:03:48,747 --> 01:03:51,416
That leaves you only 99 more to go.

601
01:03:54,461 --> 01:03:56,418
Have you not met Will Turner?

602
01:03:56,546 --> 01:03:59,332
He's noble, heroic,
a terrific soprano.

603
01:03:59,466 --> 01:04:01,791
Worth at least four.

604
01:04:01,926 --> 01:04:04,132
Maybe three and a half.

605
01:04:04,971 --> 01:04:07,924
And did I happen to mention

606
01:04:08,057 --> 01:04:10,464
he's in love?

607
01:04:11,352 --> 01:04:13,925
With a girl.

608
01:04:14,063 --> 01:04:16,435
Due to be married.

609
01:04:16,565 --> 01:04:18,474
Betrothed.

610
01:04:19,485 --> 01:04:24,146
Dividing him from her and her from him
would only be half as cruel

611
01:04:24,281 --> 01:04:29,156
as actually allowing them
to be joined in holy matrimony. Eh?

612
01:04:33,122 --> 01:04:37,949
I keep the boy. 99 souls.

613
01:04:38,085 --> 01:04:40,706
But I wonder, Sparrow,

614
01:04:40,838 --> 01:04:43,044
can you live with this?

615
01:04:43,799 --> 01:04:47,582
Can you condemn an innocent man,
a friend,

616
01:04:47,720 --> 01:04:52,630
to a lifetime of servitude in your name
while you roam free?

617
01:04:55,894 --> 01:04:57,886
Yep. I'm good with it.

618
01:04:58,021 --> 01:05:01,140
Shall we seal it in blood?
I mean ink.

619
01:05:05,486 --> 01:05:08,190
Three days.

620
01:05:12,743 --> 01:05:14,865
Three days.

621
01:05:22,502 --> 01:05:25,372
- Mr Gibbs.
- Aye.

622
01:05:25,505 --> 01:05:28,078
I feel sullied and unusual.

623
01:05:28,216 --> 01:05:33,969
And how do you intend to harvest
these 99 souls in three days?

624
01:05:34,096 --> 01:05:40,098
Fortunately, he was mum as to
the condition in which these souls need be.

625
01:05:40,227 --> 01:05:42,136
Tortuga.

626
01:05:43,272 --> 01:05:44,682
Tortuga.

627
01:05:48,735 --> 01:05:52,317
It's an outrage.
Port tariffs, berthing fees,

628
01:05:52,447 --> 01:05:55,650
wharf handling
and, heaven help us, pilotage.

629
01:05:55,783 --> 01:06:00,112
Are we all to work for
the East lndia Trading Company, then?

630
01:06:00,246 --> 01:06:04,658
I'm afraid, sir, Tortuga is
the only free port left in these waters.

631
01:06:04,792 --> 01:06:07,365
A pirate port is what you mean.
Well, I'm sorry.

632
01:06:07,503 --> 01:06:12,923
An honest sailor is what I am. I make
my living fair and I sleep well each night.

633
01:06:13,675 --> 01:06:15,548
Sir!

634
01:06:38,866 --> 01:06:41,356
She wants you to do something.

635
01:06:44,538 --> 01:06:47,159
She's trying to give a sign.

636
01:06:55,590 --> 01:06:58,045
Over there! Look for a sign!

637
01:07:02,847 --> 01:07:04,969
Look, there! There it is!

638
01:07:05,474 --> 01:07:08,011
- There's the sign!
- That's seaweed.

639
01:07:08,143 --> 01:07:11,179
- Seaweed can be a sign.
- Looks like entrails.

640
01:07:11,313 --> 01:07:16,354
- That would be a bad sign.
- What's that over there?

641
01:07:34,710 --> 01:07:37,876
And what makes you think
you're worthy to crew the Black Pearl?

642
01:07:38,005 --> 01:07:40,211
I've never sailed a day in me life.

643
01:07:40,340 --> 01:07:43,459
I figure I should get out,
see the world while I'm still young.

644
01:07:43,593 --> 01:07:46,926
You'll do. Make your mark.
Next!

645
01:07:47,055 --> 01:07:49,676
My wife ran off with my dog

646
01:07:50,183 --> 01:07:52,590
and I'm drunk for a month

647
01:07:52,727 --> 01:07:54,766
and I don't give an ass rat's

648
01:07:54,895 --> 01:07:56,804
if I live or die.

649
01:07:56,939 --> 01:07:59,939
- Perfect. Next!
- I know what I want. I know what I want...

650
01:08:00,067 --> 01:08:04,146
- Me have one arm and a bum leg.
- It's the crow's nest for you.

651
01:08:04,279 --> 01:08:06,401
- I know what I want.
- Next!

652
01:08:06,531 --> 01:08:12,117
Ever since I was a little lad,
I've always wanted to sail the seas forever.

653
01:08:12,245 --> 01:08:14,914
Sooner than you think.
Sign the roster.

654
01:08:15,039 --> 01:08:17,280
Thanks very much.

655
01:08:17,417 --> 01:08:22,541
- How we going?
- lncluding those four, that gives us four.

656
01:08:26,050 --> 01:08:28,172
And what's your story?

657
01:08:28,302 --> 01:08:30,757
My story.

658
01:08:30,888 --> 01:08:35,929
It's exactly the same as your story,
just one chapter behind.

659
01:08:36,059 --> 01:08:39,641
I chased a man
across the seven seas.

660
01:08:39,771 --> 01:08:42,557
The pursuit cost me my crew,

661
01:08:42,690 --> 01:08:45,311
my commission,

662
01:08:45,443 --> 01:08:47,565
and my life.

663
01:08:52,658 --> 01:08:57,450
- Commodore?
- No, not any more. Weren't you listening?

664
01:09:00,499 --> 01:09:03,036
I nearly had you all off Tripoli.

665
01:09:03,835 --> 01:09:08,330
I would have,
if not for the hurricane.

666
01:09:08,465 --> 01:09:09,793
Lord.

667
01:09:09,924 --> 01:09:11,881
You didn't try to sail through it?

668
01:09:12,010 --> 01:09:14,500
So, do I make your crew

669
01:09:14,637 --> 01:09:16,594
or not?

670
01:09:17,473 --> 01:09:20,177
You haven't said where you're going.

671
01:09:20,309 --> 01:09:22,348
Somewhere nice?!

672
01:09:23,437 --> 01:09:25,809
So, am I worthy to serve

673
01:09:25,939 --> 01:09:28,690
under Captain Jack Sparrow?

674
01:09:31,194 --> 01:09:34,562
Or should I just kill you now?

675
01:09:40,745 --> 01:09:42,867
You're hired.

676
01:09:42,997 --> 01:09:46,946
Sorry. Old habits and all that.

677
01:09:47,084 --> 01:09:50,583
- Easy, sailor!
- That's our captain you're threatening.

678
01:10:03,808 --> 01:10:05,765
- Time to go.
- Aye!

679
01:10:50,435 --> 01:10:52,392
Thanks, mate.

680
01:10:58,151 --> 01:11:00,024
Carry on.

681
01:11:07,368 --> 01:11:09,905
Come on, then! Who wants some?

682
01:11:10,037 --> 01:11:13,488
Form an orderly line
and I'll have you all, one by one.

683
01:11:13,624 --> 01:11:15,865
Come on, who's first?

684
01:11:18,545 --> 01:11:22,044
I just wanted the pleasure
of doing that myself.

685
01:11:48,740 --> 01:11:51,409
James Norrington.

686
01:11:52,327 --> 01:11:55,327
What has the world done to you?

687
01:12:22,939 --> 01:12:25,145
Heave!

688
01:12:25,441 --> 01:12:27,599
Heave!

689
01:12:28,319 --> 01:12:30,276
Heave!

690
01:12:30,571 --> 01:12:32,444
Heave!

691
01:12:33,532 --> 01:12:36,947
- Heave!
- Heave!

692
01:12:39,872 --> 01:12:43,821
Secure the mast tackle, Mr Turner!

693
01:12:46,878 --> 01:12:49,000
Set to it!

694
01:12:54,343 --> 01:12:57,213
- Step aside.
- Hey! Mind yourself!

695
01:12:57,680 --> 01:13:00,052
Let go, boy!

696
01:13:02,726 --> 01:13:04,848
No.

697
01:13:12,861 --> 01:13:15,482
Haul that weevil to his feet.

698
01:13:21,077 --> 01:13:24,492
Five lashes will remind you
to stay on 'em!

699
01:13:24,622 --> 01:13:29,663
- No!
- lmpeding me in my duties?

700
01:13:29,793 --> 01:13:33,576
- You'll share the punishment.
- I'll take it all.

701
01:13:33,714 --> 01:13:36,039
Will you, now?

702
01:13:36,174 --> 01:13:41,380
And what would prompt
such an act of charity?

703
01:13:45,141 --> 01:13:47,050
My son.

704
01:13:52,023 --> 01:13:54,311
He's my son.

705
01:14:05,577 --> 01:14:08,826
What fortuitous circumstance be this?

706
01:14:08,955 --> 01:14:12,786
Five lashes be owed, I believe it is.

707
01:14:17,755 --> 01:14:20,210
No. No, I won't!

708
01:14:20,341 --> 01:14:23,092
The cat's out of the bag, Mr Turner.

709
01:14:23,552 --> 01:14:28,972
Your issue will feel its sting,
be it by the bosun's hand or your own.

710
01:14:32,853 --> 01:14:35,178
No.

711
01:14:35,313 --> 01:14:37,270
- Bosun!
- No!

712
01:15:21,523 --> 01:15:24,891
You had it easy, boy!

713
01:15:25,027 --> 01:15:27,944
- Will.
- I don't need your help.

714
01:15:29,739 --> 01:15:36,026
The bosun prides himself on cleaving flesh
from bone, with every swing.

715
01:15:36,162 --> 01:15:41,072
So I'm to understand
what you did was an act of compassion?

716
01:15:41,209 --> 01:15:43,284
Yes.

717
01:15:49,300 --> 01:15:55,135
1 00 years before the mast,
losing who you were, bit by bit,

718
01:15:55,263 --> 01:15:59,094
until you end up...
end up like poor Wyvern here.

719
01:16:00,935 --> 01:16:04,932
Once you've sworn an oath
to the Dutchman, there's no leaving it.

720
01:16:05,064 --> 01:16:08,100
Not until your debt is paid.

721
01:16:08,234 --> 01:16:10,641
I've sworn no oath.

722
01:16:12,405 --> 01:16:14,527
You must get away.

723
01:16:15,616 --> 01:16:17,691
Not until I find this.

724
01:16:19,411 --> 01:16:21,237
The key.

725
01:16:28,503 --> 01:16:30,875
The dead man's chest.

726
01:16:31,798 --> 01:16:34,004
What do you know of this?

727
01:16:34,133 --> 01:16:39,174
Open the chest with the key,
and stab the heart...

728
01:16:39,472 --> 01:16:42,721
No, don't stab the heart.

729
01:16:42,850 --> 01:16:48,140
The Dutchman needs a living heart
or there'll be no captain,

730
01:16:48,272 --> 01:16:52,767
and if there's no captain,
there's no one to have the key.

731
01:16:52,776 --> 01:16:55,313
So the captain has the key?

732
01:16:55,820 --> 01:16:58,606
- Where is the key?
- Hidden.

733
01:16:59,199 --> 01:17:01,985
- Where is the chest?
- Hidden.

734
01:17:05,079 --> 01:17:06,905
(Elizabeth) Captain Sparrow!

735
01:17:07,164 --> 01:17:09,571
Come to join me crew, lad?
Welcome aboard.

736
01:17:09,792 --> 01:17:12,662
I'm here to find the man I love.

737
01:17:13,295 --> 01:17:17,921
I'm deeply flattered, son,
but my first and only love is the sea.

738
01:17:18,508 --> 01:17:22,837
Meaning William Turner,
Captain Sparrow.

739
01:17:23,054 --> 01:17:24,298
Elizabeth.

740
01:17:24,514 --> 01:17:27,087
Hide the rum.

741
01:17:27,225 --> 01:17:30,225
These clothes do not flatter you.
It should be a dress or nothing.

742
01:17:30,478 --> 01:17:33,644
- I happen to have no dress in my cabin.
- Jack.

743
01:17:34,148 --> 01:17:37,516
I know Will came to find you.
Where is he?

744
01:17:37,943 --> 01:17:41,726
Darling, I am truly unhappy
to have to tell you this, but...

745
01:17:42,114 --> 01:17:49,279
through unfortunate circumstances
that had nothing whatsoever to do with me,

746
01:17:49,663 --> 01:17:54,455
poor Will has been press-ganged
into Davy Jones's crew.

747
01:17:54,626 --> 01:17:56,583
Davy Jones?

748
01:17:56,753 --> 01:17:57,915
(splutters)

749
01:17:58,296 --> 01:18:00,751
Oh, please.

750
01:18:01,841 --> 01:18:04,462
The captain of the Flying Dutchman?

751
01:18:04,677 --> 01:18:08,221
- You look awful. What are you doing here?
- You hired me.

752
01:18:08,222 --> 01:18:10,594
I can't help it if your standards are lax.

753
01:18:10,682 --> 01:18:13,635
- You smell funny.
- Jack!

754
01:18:13,810 --> 01:18:16,810
All I want is to find Will.

755
01:18:19,149 --> 01:18:23,857
Are you certain?
Is that what you really want most?

756
01:18:24,654 --> 01:18:26,942
Of course.

757
01:18:27,240 --> 01:18:31,700
Because I would think you'd want to find
a way to save Will most.

758
01:18:31,786 --> 01:18:35,949
- And you'd have a way of doing that?
- Well...

759
01:18:36,123 --> 01:18:39,326
- there is a chest.
- Oh, dear.

760
01:18:39,376 --> 01:18:41,831
A chest of unknown size and origin.

761
01:18:42,087 --> 01:18:47,163
What contains
the still-beating heart of Davy Jones.

762
01:18:48,426 --> 01:18:50,501
And whoever possesses that chest

763
01:18:50,637 --> 01:18:54,764
possesses the leverage to command Jones
to do whatever it is he or she wants,

764
01:18:55,099 --> 01:19:00,389
including saving brave William
from his grim fate.

765
01:19:00,688 --> 01:19:04,471
You don't actually believe him, do you?

766
01:19:07,319 --> 01:19:09,774
How would we find it?

767
01:19:12,157 --> 01:19:14,030
With this.

768
01:19:14,242 --> 01:19:17,195
My compass... is unique.

769
01:19:17,620 --> 01:19:20,988
"Unique" here
having the meaning of "broken."

770
01:19:21,541 --> 01:19:26,202
True enough,
this compass does not point north.

771
01:19:28,172 --> 01:19:31,256
Where does it point?

772
01:19:31,717 --> 01:19:36,674
It points to the thing
you want most in this world.

773
01:19:39,224 --> 01:19:41,263
Oh, Jack.

774
01:19:42,977 --> 01:19:46,808
- Are you telling the truth?
- Every word, love.

775
01:19:47,023 --> 01:19:49,644
And what you want most in this world

776
01:19:50,276 --> 01:19:55,352
is to find the chest of Davy Jones,
is it not?

777
01:19:55,489 --> 01:20:00,565
- To save Will.
- By finding the chest of Davy Jones.

778
01:20:13,131 --> 01:20:15,621
- Mr. Gibbs!
- Cap'n.

779
01:20:16,008 --> 01:20:18,380
- We have our heading.
- Finally!

780
01:20:18,427 --> 01:20:23,302
Cast off those lines.
Weigh anchor and crowd that canvas!

781
01:20:23,641 --> 01:20:27,056
- Miss Swann.
- (Gibb shouts orders to crew)

782
01:20:28,479 --> 01:20:31,894
Welcome to the crew,
former commodore.

783
01:20:38,321 --> 01:20:43,362
There's something to knowing the exact
shape of the world and one's place in it.

784
01:20:43,534 --> 01:20:46,238
I assure you, these are not necessary.

785
01:20:46,662 --> 01:20:51,204
I thought you'd be interested
in the whereabouts of your daughter.

786
01:20:51,625 --> 01:20:55,325
- You have news of her?
- Most recently seen on Tortuga,

787
01:20:55,671 --> 01:20:59,798
and then left in the company
of a known pirate, Jack Sparrow,

788
01:20:59,966 --> 01:21:02,966
and other fugitives from justice.

789
01:21:03,220 --> 01:21:05,093
"Justice"? Hardly.

790
01:21:05,180 --> 01:21:11,099
Including the previous owner of this sword,
I believe.

791
01:21:11,436 --> 01:21:14,313
Our ships are in pursuit. Justice will
be dispensed by cannonade and cutlass,

792
01:21:14,313 --> 01:21:18,392
and all manner
of remorseless pieces of metal.

793
01:21:18,609 --> 01:21:24,812
I find it distasteful to even contemplate
the horror facing all those on board.

794
01:21:27,784 --> 01:21:29,610
What do you want from me?

795
01:21:29,744 --> 01:21:33,077
Your authority as governor,
your influence in London,

796
01:21:33,248 --> 01:21:36,830
and your loyalty
to the East lndia Trading Company.

797
01:21:37,543 --> 01:21:40,329
To you, you mean.

798
01:21:44,091 --> 01:21:46,877
Shall I remove these shackles?

799
01:21:50,180 --> 01:21:53,762
Do what you can for my daughter.

800
01:21:59,189 --> 01:22:04,313
(Beckett) So you see, Mercer, every man
has a price he will willingly accept.

801
01:22:04,736 --> 01:22:08,650
Even for what he hoped never to sell.

802
01:22:15,246 --> 01:22:18,330
I wager ten years.

803
01:22:19,250 --> 01:22:21,538
I'll match ten years.

804
01:22:22,587 --> 01:22:24,246
Agreed.

805
01:22:27,300 --> 01:22:30,004
Wondering how it's played?

806
01:22:30,136 --> 01:22:31,962
- I understand.
- Liar!

807
01:22:32,096 --> 01:22:37,635
It's a game of deception. But your bet
includes all the dice, not just your own.

808
01:22:38,060 --> 01:22:41,060
- What are they wagering?
- The only thing we have.

809
01:22:41,188 --> 01:22:43,513
Years of service.

810
01:22:45,692 --> 01:22:49,855
- So any crew member can be challenged?
- Aye. Anyone.

811
01:22:52,782 --> 01:22:55,486
I challenge Davy Jones.

812
01:23:15,178 --> 01:23:18,095
I accept, mate.

813
01:23:29,692 --> 01:23:35,196
- The stakes?
- My soul. An eternity of servitude.

814
01:23:35,322 --> 01:23:37,480
No!

815
01:23:39,701 --> 01:23:41,195
Against?

816
01:23:42,704 --> 01:23:44,661
I want this.

817
01:23:56,842 --> 01:24:01,717
- How do you know of the key?
- That's not part of the game, is it?

818
01:24:04,933 --> 01:24:07,506
You can still walk away.

819
01:24:32,250 --> 01:24:35,286
- What's this?
- I'm in. Matching his wager.

820
01:24:35,420 --> 01:24:38,290
No! Don't do this.

821
01:24:40,258 --> 01:24:43,009
The die is cast.

822
01:24:43,136 --> 01:24:46,172
I bid three twos.

823
01:24:46,305 --> 01:24:48,427
It's your bid, Captain.

824
01:24:54,771 --> 01:24:57,724
Four fours.

825
01:25:06,866 --> 01:25:09,950
Four fives.

826
01:25:10,078 --> 01:25:11,737
Six threes.

827
01:25:21,463 --> 01:25:24,582
Seven fives.

828
01:25:48,530 --> 01:25:50,771
Eight fives.

829
01:25:55,537 --> 01:25:58,988
Welcome to the crew, lad.

830
01:26:01,209 --> 01:26:04,162
Twelve fives.

831
01:26:04,462 --> 01:26:06,086
Twelve fives.

832
01:26:06,214 --> 01:26:09,000
Call me a liar, or up the bid.

833
01:26:10,384 --> 01:26:13,918
And be called a liar myself
for my trouble?

834
01:26:17,432 --> 01:26:22,259
Bootstrap Bill, you're a liar
and you will spend an eternity on this ship.

835
01:26:24,231 --> 01:26:28,394
Master Turner,
feel free to go ashore

836
01:26:29,736 --> 01:26:32,820
the very next time we make port!

837
01:26:40,454 --> 01:26:43,953
Fool. Why did you do that?

838
01:26:45,542 --> 01:26:47,700
I couldn't let you lose.

839
01:26:47,836 --> 01:26:50,540
It was never about winning or losing.

840
01:26:52,966 --> 01:26:54,376
The key.

841
01:26:56,094 --> 01:26:58,928
You just wanted to know where it was.

842
01:27:18,573 --> 01:27:21,146
Captain says I'm to relieve you.

843
01:27:25,204 --> 01:27:27,243
Captain's orders.

844
01:29:17,101 --> 01:29:19,638
Here. Take this, too.

845
01:29:20,646 --> 01:29:23,979
Now, get yourself to land, and stay there.

846
01:29:24,483 --> 01:29:27,686
It was always in my blood to die at sea.

847
01:29:27,820 --> 01:29:30,820
It was not a fate I ever wanted for you.

848
01:29:35,619 --> 01:29:39,283
It's not a fate you had to choose
for yourself, either.

849
01:29:39,414 --> 01:29:45,582
Aye. I could say I did what I had to,
when I left you to go pirating.

850
01:29:46,546 --> 01:29:50,839
But it would taste a lie
to say it wasn't what I wanted.

851
01:29:52,593 --> 01:29:55,214
You owe me nothing, Will.

852
01:29:55,346 --> 01:29:57,504
Now go.

853
01:29:58,682 --> 01:30:01,303
They'll know you helped me.

854
01:30:02,728 --> 01:30:04,685
What more can they do to me?

855
01:30:10,568 --> 01:30:12,774
I take this with a promise.

856
01:30:12,904 --> 01:30:15,276
I'll find a way to sever
Jones's hold on you,

857
01:30:15,406 --> 01:30:19,237
and not rest until this blade
pierces his heart.

858
01:30:20,786 --> 01:30:23,074
I will not abandon you.

859
01:30:25,749 --> 01:30:28,286
I promise.

860
01:30:42,473 --> 01:30:44,548
- Beckett?
- Yes, they're signed.

861
01:30:44,684 --> 01:30:48,681
Lord Cutler Beckett
of the East lndia Trading Company.

862
01:30:48,812 --> 01:30:52,512
Will was working for Beckett
and never said a word.

863
01:30:52,941 --> 01:30:56,689
Beckett wants the compass.
Only one reason for that.

864
01:30:56,820 --> 01:30:59,310
Of course.

865
01:30:59,447 --> 01:31:03,147
- He wants the chest.
- Yes, he did say something about a chest.

866
01:31:03,284 --> 01:31:06,866
If the company controls the chest,
they controls the sea.

867
01:31:06,996 --> 01:31:10,696
- A truly discomfiting notion, love.
- And bad.

868
01:31:10,833 --> 01:31:14,450
Bad for every mother's son
what calls himself a pirate.

869
01:31:14,587 --> 01:31:17,790
I think there's a bit more speed
to be coaxed from these sails.

870
01:31:17,923 --> 01:31:19,998
Brace the foreyard!

871
01:31:20,134 --> 01:31:23,300
Might I enquire
as to how you came by these?

872
01:31:23,428 --> 01:31:25,301
- Persuasion.
- Friendly?

873
01:31:25,430 --> 01:31:27,007
Decidedly not.

874
01:31:27,140 --> 01:31:32,679
Will strikes a deal, yet you were the one
with the prize - full pardon.

875
01:31:32,812 --> 01:31:35,100
"Commission as a privateer
on behalf of England

876
01:31:35,231 --> 01:31:37,900
and the East lndia Trading Company."

877
01:31:38,025 --> 01:31:40,266
As if I could be bought
for such a low price.

878
01:31:40,403 --> 01:31:42,858
- Jack, the letters, give them back.
- No.

879
01:31:42,988 --> 01:31:45,276
Persuade me.

880
01:31:45,407 --> 01:31:49,155
You do know Will taught me
how to handle a sword?

881
01:31:51,580 --> 01:31:55,244
As I said, persuade me.

882
01:32:09,555 --> 01:32:11,962
It's a curious thing.

883
01:32:13,100 --> 01:32:18,722
There was a time I would have wanted you
to look like that while thinking about me.

884
01:32:18,855 --> 01:32:21,180
- I don't know what you mean.
- I think you do.

885
01:32:21,316 --> 01:32:24,102
Don't be absurd.
I trust him, that's all.

886
01:32:28,823 --> 01:32:31,064
So you never wondered
how your latest fiance

887
01:32:31,200 --> 01:32:35,197
ended up on the Flying Dutchman
in the first place?

888
01:32:59,727 --> 01:33:02,976
Strange to come upon a longboat
so far out in open water.

889
01:33:03,105 --> 01:33:06,473
Just put as many leagues behind us
as you can. As fast as you can.

890
01:33:06,609 --> 01:33:09,693
And what are we running from?

891
01:33:14,533 --> 01:33:17,284
That dress. Where did you get it?

892
01:33:17,410 --> 01:33:19,069
It was found aboard the ship.

893
01:33:19,204 --> 01:33:23,664
The crew thought it was a spirit
bringing some omen of ill-fate.

894
01:33:23,791 --> 01:33:27,159
- That's foolish.
- Yes. Exceedingly foolish.

895
01:33:27,295 --> 01:33:33,249
It brought good fortune.
The spirit told us, "Pull in at Tortuga",

896
01:33:33,384 --> 01:33:35,341
and we made a nice bit of profit there.

897
01:33:35,469 --> 01:33:38,553
Off the books, of course.

898
01:33:38,680 --> 01:33:42,428
I imagine some of your crew
may have jumped ship there.

899
01:33:42,559 --> 01:33:45,393
- Why do you ask?
- Captain! A ship's been spotted.

900
01:33:45,520 --> 01:33:48,306
- Colours?
- She isn't flying any.

901
01:33:48,440 --> 01:33:50,397
- Pirates.
- Or worse.

902
01:34:01,744 --> 01:34:05,278
You will watch this.

903
01:34:12,465 --> 01:34:15,418
Let no joyful voice be heard!

904
01:34:17,597 --> 01:34:20,763
Let no man look up at the sky with hope.

905
01:34:23,063 --> 01:34:28,768
And let this day be cursed
by we who ready to wake

906
01:34:30,281 --> 01:34:32,403
the kraken.

907
01:34:32,868 --> 01:34:34,492
No!

908
01:34:51,433 --> 01:34:53,923
I've doomed us all.

909
01:34:54,437 --> 01:34:57,223
It's the Flying Dutchman!

910
01:35:02,197 --> 01:35:04,569
Mother Carey's chickens!
What happened?

911
01:35:04,701 --> 01:35:07,191
Must have hit a reef.

912
01:35:14,880 --> 01:35:17,880
Free the rudder!
Hard to port then hard to starboard.

913
01:35:18,010 --> 01:35:21,461
- Free the rudder!
- Hard to port!

914
01:35:28,773 --> 01:35:32,224
- Kraken!
- Kraken!

915
01:35:45,754 --> 01:35:48,671
Here it is! Take it!

916
01:35:48,801 --> 01:35:50,758
Take it!

917
01:38:02,767 --> 01:38:07,262
The boy's not here.
He must have been claimed by the sea.

918
01:38:07,399 --> 01:38:09,854
I am the sea.

919
01:38:13,573 --> 01:38:16,776
You need time alone with your thoughts.

920
01:38:16,911 --> 01:38:19,401
- Brig!
- What of the survivors?

921
01:38:24,963 --> 01:38:28,296
There are no survivors.

922
01:38:48,535 --> 01:38:52,366
The chest is no longer safe.
Chart a course to lsla Cruces.

923
01:38:52,499 --> 01:38:55,168
Get me there first,
or there'll be the devil to pay.

924
01:38:55,295 --> 01:38:57,086
"First"?

925
01:38:57,214 --> 01:39:00,380
Who sent that thieving charlatan
onto my ship?

926
01:39:00,510 --> 01:39:02,419
Who told him of the key?

927
01:39:05,600 --> 01:39:07,722
Jack Sparrow.

928
01:39:26,127 --> 01:39:32,876
My intuitive sense of the female creature
informs me that you are troubled.

929
01:39:33,010 --> 01:39:36,378
I just thought I'd be married by now.

930
01:39:36,516 --> 01:39:38,804
I'm so ready to be married.

931
01:39:46,403 --> 01:39:48,395
You know...

932
01:39:50,074 --> 01:39:55,447
Lizzie, I am captain of a ship,

933
01:39:55,581 --> 01:40:04,038
and being captain of a ship, I could in fact
perform a "marr-i-age" right here.

934
01:40:04,176 --> 01:40:06,501
Right on this deck.

935
01:40:06,638 --> 01:40:08,547
Right now.

936
01:40:12,228 --> 01:40:14,469
No, thank you.

937
01:40:14,607 --> 01:40:16,101
Why not?

938
01:40:16,234 --> 01:40:19,934
We are very much alike, you and l.
I and you. Us.

939
01:40:20,072 --> 01:40:24,401
Except for a sense of honour
and decency and a moral centre.

940
01:40:24,912 --> 01:40:27,284
And personal hygiene.

941
01:40:28,792 --> 01:40:31,033
Trifles.

942
01:40:31,170 --> 01:40:33,660
You will come over to my side. I know it.

943
01:40:33,799 --> 01:40:39,041
- You seem very certain.
- One word, love. Curiosity.

944
01:40:39,181 --> 01:40:40,758
You long for freedom.

945
01:40:40,891 --> 01:40:43,927
You long to do what you want to do
because you want it.

946
01:40:44,062 --> 01:40:47,098
To act on selfish impulse.

947
01:40:47,233 --> 01:40:49,937
You want to see what it's like.

948
01:40:50,070 --> 01:40:54,482
One day, you won't be able to resist.

949
01:40:55,076 --> 01:40:57,613
Why doesn't your compass work?

950
01:40:59,290 --> 01:41:02,326
My compass works fine.

951
01:41:02,795 --> 01:41:04,538
Because you and I are alike,

952
01:41:04,672 --> 01:41:07,875
and there will come a moment
when you have a chance to show it.

953
01:41:08,010 --> 01:41:11,259
- To do the right thing.
- I love those moments.

954
01:41:11,806 --> 01:41:14,047
I like to wave at them as they pass by.

955
01:41:14,185 --> 01:41:19,724
You'll have the chance to do something...
something courageous.

956
01:41:19,859 --> 01:41:22,978
And when you do,
you'll discover something.

957
01:41:23,113 --> 01:41:25,650
That you're a good man.

958
01:41:25,783 --> 01:41:28,238
All evidence to the contrary.

959
01:41:28,370 --> 01:41:31,370
I have faith in you.

960
01:41:31,499 --> 01:41:34,369
- Want to know why?
- Do tell, dearie.

961
01:41:36,214 --> 01:41:39,167
Curiosity.

962
01:41:39,301 --> 01:41:42,218
You're going to want it.

963
01:41:42,346 --> 01:41:46,177
A chance to be admired
and gain the rewards that follow.

964
01:41:47,562 --> 01:41:49,684
You won't be able to resist.

965
01:41:51,108 --> 01:41:56,268
You're going to want to know
what it tastes like.

966
01:41:58,868 --> 01:42:01,821
I do want to know what it tastes like.

967
01:42:01,955 --> 01:42:03,698
But, seeing as you're a good man,

968
01:42:03,833 --> 01:42:08,210
I know that you'd never put me in a position
that would compromise my honour.

969
01:42:16,558 --> 01:42:18,633
I'm proud of you, Jack.

970
01:42:18,769 --> 01:42:21,555
Land, ho!

971
01:42:26,780 --> 01:42:28,571
I want my jar of dirt.

972
01:42:30,076 --> 01:42:32,946
- You're pulling too fast.
- You're pulling too slow.

973
01:42:33,079 --> 01:42:38,155
- We don't want the kraken to catch us.
- I'm saving me strength for when it comes.

974
01:42:38,295 --> 01:42:42,672
And I don't think it's "kracken" anyways.
I always heard it said "krayken".

975
01:42:42,801 --> 01:42:44,378
With a long a?

976
01:42:44,511 --> 01:42:48,342
No, no, no, no, no, no, no. "Krocken"
is how it's pronounced in Scandinavian,

977
01:42:48,475 --> 01:42:50,182
and "kracken" is closer to that.

978
01:42:50,310 --> 01:42:53,725
We ain't Scandinavians, are we?
"Krayken."

979
01:42:53,857 --> 01:42:56,526
It's a mythological creature.
I can calls it what I wants.

980
01:43:00,449 --> 01:43:02,655
Guard the boat. Mind the tide.

981
01:43:02,786 --> 01:43:05,074
Don't touch my dirt.

982
01:43:41,002 --> 01:43:46,162
This doesn't work. And it certainly
doesn't show you what you want most.

983
01:43:50,390 --> 01:43:53,675
Yes, it does. You're sitting on it.

984
01:43:54,603 --> 01:43:56,262
- Beg pardon?
- Move.

985
01:44:04,033 --> 01:44:06,784
- "Guard the boat!" "Mind the tide!"
- I could join the circus.

986
01:44:06,911 --> 01:44:08,488
Mind if I shine your shoes, sir?

987
01:44:12,961 --> 01:44:17,622
They're here. And I cannot step foot
on land again for near of a decade.

988
01:44:17,758 --> 01:44:19,715
You'll trust us to act in your stead?

989
01:44:19,844 --> 01:44:23,177
I'll trust you to know
what awaits you should you fail!

990
01:44:24,809 --> 01:44:27,762
- Down, then!
- Down!

991
01:45:37,530 --> 01:45:39,569
It's real.

992
01:45:40,575 --> 01:45:42,614
You actually were telling the truth.

993
01:45:42,745 --> 01:45:46,244
I do that quite a lot,
yet people are always surprised.

994
01:45:46,375 --> 01:45:49,209
With good reason!

995
01:45:49,337 --> 01:45:51,744
Will!

996
01:45:51,882 --> 01:45:55,630
You're all right! Thank God!
I came to find you!

997
01:46:01,728 --> 01:46:03,803
How did you get here?

998
01:46:03,939 --> 01:46:07,853
Sea turtles, mate.
A pair of them, strapped to my feet.

999
01:46:07,987 --> 01:46:09,813
Not so easy, is it?

1000
01:46:09,947 --> 01:46:12,520
- But I do owe you thanks, Jack.
- You do?

1001
01:46:12,659 --> 01:46:16,786
After you tricked me onto that ship
to square your debt with Jones...

1002
01:46:16,915 --> 01:46:18,907
- What?
- What?

1003
01:46:19,543 --> 01:46:22,662
..I was reunited with my father.

1004
01:46:22,797 --> 01:46:24,955
Well, you're welcome, then.

1005
01:46:25,509 --> 01:46:29,292
Everything you said to me,
every word was a lie!

1006
01:46:29,431 --> 01:46:30,889
Pretty much.

1007
01:46:31,017 --> 01:46:33,342
Time and tide, love.

1008
01:46:33,478 --> 01:46:35,684
Oi! What are you doing?

1009
01:46:36,816 --> 01:46:39,141
I'm going to kill Jones.

1010
01:46:40,195 --> 01:46:42,436
Can't let you do that, William.

1011
01:46:42,573 --> 01:46:48,575
Cos if Jones is dead, who's to call
his terrible beastie off the hunt, eh?

1012
01:46:54,672 --> 01:46:57,957
Now, if you please.

1013
01:46:58,678 --> 01:47:01,168
The key.

1014
01:47:03,392 --> 01:47:06,061
I keep the promises I make, Jack.

1015
01:47:06,187 --> 01:47:09,472
I intend to free my father.
I hope you're here to see it.

1016
01:47:09,609 --> 01:47:13,191
I can't let you do that either.
So sorry.

1017
01:47:13,322 --> 01:47:16,904
I knew you'd warm up to me eventually.

1018
01:47:17,786 --> 01:47:21,700
Lord Beckett desires
the content of that chest.

1019
01:47:21,833 --> 01:47:24,917
I deliver it, I get my life back.

1020
01:47:26,548 --> 01:47:28,042
The dark side of ambition.

1021
01:47:28,174 --> 01:47:31,874
I prefer to see it
as the promise of redemption.

1022
01:47:36,227 --> 01:47:37,721
Stop it!

1023
01:47:44,488 --> 01:47:45,946
Will!

1024
01:47:46,991 --> 01:47:50,075
- Guard the chest!
- No!

1025
01:47:50,204 --> 01:47:55,790
This is barbaric!
This is no way for grown men to...

1026
01:47:55,919 --> 01:48:00,664
Fine! Let's just haul out our swords
and start banging away at each other.

1027
01:48:00,801 --> 01:48:03,374
That will solve everything! I've had it!

1028
01:48:03,513 --> 01:48:08,423
I've had it with wobbly-legged,
rum-soaked pirates!

1029
01:48:11,105 --> 01:48:16,229
- How'd this go all screwy?
- Well, each wants the chest for hisself.

1030
01:48:16,362 --> 01:48:19,481
Mr Norrington, I think,
is trying to regain a bit of honour,

1031
01:48:19,617 --> 01:48:22,072
old Jack's looking to trade it,
save his own skin,

1032
01:48:22,203 --> 01:48:26,330
then Turner, there, I think he's trying
to settle some unresolved business

1033
01:48:26,459 --> 01:48:30,041
'twixt him and his twice-cursed
pirate father.

1034
01:48:30,172 --> 01:48:32,081
- Sad.
- This is madness!

1035
01:48:32,217 --> 01:48:35,965
That chest must be worth
more than a shiny penny.

1036
01:48:36,097 --> 01:48:37,970
Terrible temptation.

1037
01:48:38,100 --> 01:48:44,054
If we was any kind of decent,
we'd remove temptation from their path.

1038
01:48:49,781 --> 01:48:51,572
Enough!

1039
01:48:57,959 --> 01:49:00,496
The heat!

1040
01:49:19,613 --> 01:49:21,652
Bugger!

1041
01:49:33,839 --> 01:49:36,294
By your leave, Mr Turner.

1042
01:50:23,279 --> 01:50:26,315
By your leave, Mr Norrington.

1043
01:51:18,476 --> 01:51:21,429
Do excuse me
while I kill the man who ruined my life.

1044
01:51:21,564 --> 01:51:25,561
- Be my guest.
- Let us examine that claim for a moment.

1045
01:51:26,028 --> 01:51:31,448
Who was it, that at the very moment you
had a notorious pirate safely behind bars,

1046
01:51:31,577 --> 01:51:38,290
saw fit to free said pirate and
take your dearly beloved all to hisself?

1047
01:51:39,504 --> 01:51:42,041
So whose fault is it, really,

1048
01:51:42,174 --> 01:51:46,503
that you've ended up a rum-pot deckhand
what takes orders from pirates?

1049
01:51:46,638 --> 01:51:48,595
Enough!

1050
01:51:50,894 --> 01:51:54,642
Unfortunately, Mr Turner, he's right!

1051
01:51:56,652 --> 01:51:58,525
Still rooting for you, mate.

1052
01:52:56,730 --> 01:52:59,516
We got it! We got it! We got it!

1053
01:53:13,919 --> 01:53:16,955
Hello, poppet.

1054
01:53:43,834 --> 01:53:45,707
Come on, run!

1055
01:54:28,601 --> 01:54:30,510
- Sword!
- Sword! Sword!

1056
01:54:33,523 --> 01:54:36,013
Sword!

1057
01:55:16,789 --> 01:55:19,362
Follow my voice.
Follow my voice.

1058
01:55:19,501 --> 01:55:21,707
To the left... no, turn around.

1059
01:55:21,837 --> 01:55:23,876
Go to the right.

1060
01:55:24,006 --> 01:55:26,461
No, that's a tree.

1061
01:55:26,593 --> 01:55:28,051
Shut it.

1062
01:56:17,660 --> 01:56:19,985
Jar of dirt!

1063
01:57:20,159 --> 01:57:22,116
Right.

1064
01:57:55,329 --> 01:57:57,570
We've got it!

1065
01:58:07,303 --> 01:58:10,303
Come on, Turner!

1066
01:58:26,495 --> 01:58:30,990
Leave him lie! Unless you plan
on using him to hit something with.

1067
01:58:31,460 --> 01:58:34,081
- We're not getting out of this.
- Not with the chest.

1068
01:58:34,214 --> 01:58:36,123
Into the boat.

1069
01:58:36,259 --> 01:58:39,176
- You're mad!
- Don't wait for me.

1070
01:58:45,395 --> 01:58:49,095
- I say we respect his final wish.
- Aye!

1071
01:58:58,538 --> 01:59:00,993
Your bravery is wasted.

1072
01:59:02,417 --> 01:59:08,704
I shall pry the chest away
from your cold, dead hands.

1073
01:59:10,637 --> 01:59:12,380
Here you go!

1074
01:59:24,196 --> 01:59:25,987
Pirates!

1075
01:59:44,097 --> 01:59:47,548
- What happened to the chest?
- Norrington took it to draw them off.

1076
01:59:48,019 --> 01:59:51,222
- You're pulling too hard!
- You ain't pulling hard enough!

1077
01:59:51,690 --> 01:59:54,690
- Where's the commodore?
- Fell behind.

1078
01:59:54,820 --> 01:59:56,942
My prayers be with him.

1079
01:59:57,072 --> 01:59:59,029
Best not wallow in our grief.

1080
01:59:59,158 --> 02:00:03,985
The bright side is you're back,
and made it off free and clear.

1081
02:00:19,393 --> 02:00:21,681
Lord on high, deliver us.

1082
02:00:24,984 --> 02:00:26,775
I'll handle this, mate.

1083
02:00:29,073 --> 02:00:30,697
Oi, fishface!

1084
02:00:32,202 --> 02:00:34,739
Lose something? Eh? Scungilli!

1085
02:00:40,046 --> 02:00:42,003
Got it!

1086
02:00:43,258 --> 02:00:46,258
Come to negotiate, eh,
have you, you slimy git?

1087
02:00:46,387 --> 02:00:47,797
Look what I got.

1088
02:00:47,930 --> 02:00:50,966
I got a jar of dirt.
I got a jar of dirt

1089
02:00:51,102 --> 02:00:52,975
And guess what's inside it?

1090
02:00:53,939 --> 02:00:55,397
Enough!

1091
02:01:03,534 --> 02:01:06,238
- Hard to starboard.
- Hard to starboard!

1092
02:01:06,371 --> 02:01:09,490
Brace up the foreyard!

1093
02:01:12,671 --> 02:01:14,663
Hard to starboard!

1094
02:01:14,799 --> 02:01:19,674
Send his beloved Pearl
back to the depths.

1095
02:01:31,780 --> 02:01:35,611
She's on us! She's on us!

1096
02:01:37,245 --> 02:01:41,289
- Let them taste the triple guns.
- Aye, Captain.

1097
02:02:07,202 --> 02:02:08,696
Make fast!

1098
02:02:09,997 --> 02:02:12,487
Go on, me darling!
Show us what you've got!

1099
02:02:12,625 --> 02:02:15,329
Come on, girl!

1100
02:02:21,428 --> 02:02:23,800
- She's falling behind!
- Aye. We've got her!

1101
02:02:23,932 --> 02:02:27,596
- We're the faster?
- Against the wind, the Dutchman beats us.

1102
02:02:27,728 --> 02:02:30,135
- But with the wind...
- We rob her advantage.

1103
02:02:30,273 --> 02:02:32,064
Aye.

1104
02:02:34,153 --> 02:02:35,647
We're out of range!

1105
02:02:35,780 --> 02:02:40,358
Break off pursuit,
run her light and douse canvas!

1106
02:02:40,494 --> 02:02:42,819
We're giving up, sir?

1107
02:02:52,970 --> 02:02:55,211
They're giving up!

1108
02:03:01,356 --> 02:03:03,431
My father is on that ship.

1109
02:03:03,567 --> 02:03:06,022
We can take her.
We should turn and fight.

1110
02:03:06,154 --> 02:03:13,069
Why fight when you can negotiate?
All one needs is the proper leverage.

1111
02:03:23,218 --> 02:03:25,969
Where is it?
Where is the thump-thump?

1112
02:03:27,389 --> 02:03:30,306
We must have hit a reef!

1113
02:03:32,438 --> 02:03:36,435
No. It's not a reef!
Get away from the rail!

1114
02:03:36,568 --> 02:03:39,568
- What is it?
- The kraken.

1115
02:03:39,990 --> 02:03:42,196
- To arms!
- Load guns! Defend the mast!

1116
02:03:42,325 --> 02:03:46,737
It'll attack the starboard. Run out
the cannons and hold for my signal!

1117
02:04:20,376 --> 02:04:22,783
Easy, boys!

1118
02:04:26,217 --> 02:04:27,212
Will?

1119
02:04:27,761 --> 02:04:29,919
Steady! Steady.

1120
02:04:31,932 --> 02:04:35,347
- Will!
- Hold. Hold.

1121
02:04:35,478 --> 02:04:38,431
I think we've held fire long enough.

1122
02:04:39,693 --> 02:04:42,018
- Will!
- Fire!

1123
02:05:02,181 --> 02:05:05,680
- lt'll be back. We have to get off the ship.
- There's no boats.

1124
02:05:13,946 --> 02:05:18,488
Pull the grates. Get all the gunpowder
onto the net in the cargo hold!

1125
02:05:20,871 --> 02:05:25,283
- Whatever you do, don't miss.
- As soon as you're clear.

1126
02:05:39,521 --> 02:05:43,600
We are short-stocked on gunpowder!
Six barrels!

1127
02:05:44,653 --> 02:05:46,728
Hurry up!

1128
02:05:46,864 --> 02:05:50,398
- There's only six kegs of powder!
- Then load the rum.

1129
02:05:59,338 --> 02:06:02,124
Aye, the rum too!

1130
02:06:30,379 --> 02:06:32,336
Step to!

1131
02:06:36,053 --> 02:06:38,590
You coward.

1132
02:06:43,480 --> 02:06:45,472
Not good.

1133
02:06:53,409 --> 02:06:56,078
- Haul away!
- Heave!

1134
02:06:56,204 --> 02:07:00,497
- Heave like you're being paid for it!
- Put your backs into it!

1135
02:07:43,642 --> 02:07:46,845
- I got you! I got you!
- Don't let me go!

1136
02:07:46,979 --> 02:07:49,600
- I won't let you go!
- Shoot me!

1137
02:07:54,948 --> 02:07:57,948
- Clear?
- Higher! Higher!

1138
02:08:14,849 --> 02:08:16,924
Here! Come on!

1139
02:08:17,644 --> 02:08:20,217
Come and get me. I'm over here!

1140
02:08:20,356 --> 02:08:22,395
Come on!

1141
02:08:47,392 --> 02:08:50,595
Shoot! Elizabeth, shoot!

1142
02:10:17,969 --> 02:10:19,842
Did we kill it?

1143
02:10:19,972 --> 02:10:23,554
No. We just made it angry.

1144
02:10:23,977 --> 02:10:26,894
We're not out of this yet.
Captain, orders!

1145
02:10:28,858 --> 02:10:31,527
Abandon ship. Into the longboat.

1146
02:10:33,823 --> 02:10:36,657
Jack. The Pearl.

1147
02:10:37,953 --> 02:10:40,278
She's only a ship, mate.

1148
02:10:40,415 --> 02:10:42,905
He's right.
We have to head for land.

1149
02:10:43,044 --> 02:10:46,080
- It's a lot of open water.
- It's a lot of water.

1150
02:10:46,214 --> 02:10:50,792
We have to try. We can get away
as it takes down the Pearl.

1151
02:10:52,764 --> 02:10:54,555
Abandon ship.

1152
02:10:54,684 --> 02:10:57,056
Abandon ship or abandon hope.

1153
02:11:13,834 --> 02:11:15,956
Thank you, Jack.

1154
02:11:18,089 --> 02:11:20,793
We're not free yet, love.

1155
02:11:20,926 --> 02:11:23,048
You came back.

1156
02:11:25,641 --> 02:11:28,310
I always knew you were a good man.

1157
02:11:41,329 --> 02:11:44,080
Prepare to cast off.
There's no time to lose.

1158
02:11:44,207 --> 02:11:46,614
Come on, Will. Step to!

1159
02:12:05,776 --> 02:12:08,183
It's after you, not the ship.

1160
02:12:08,322 --> 02:12:11,192
It's not us.

1161
02:12:11,325 --> 02:12:14,076
This is the only way, don't you see?

1162
02:12:17,418 --> 02:12:19,540
I'm not sorry.

1163
02:12:25,135 --> 02:12:27,092
Pirate.

1164
02:12:46,539 --> 02:12:48,697
Where's Jack?

1165
02:12:52,338 --> 02:12:56,252
He elected to stay behind
to give us a chance.

1166
02:13:04,979 --> 02:13:06,770
Go!

1167
02:13:19,582 --> 02:13:21,907
Bugger. Bugger, bugger.

1168
02:13:51,290 --> 02:13:53,365
Come on!

1169
02:13:57,673 --> 02:14:00,377
Just got it. Come on.

1170
02:14:29,507 --> 02:14:31,914
Not so bad.

1171
02:14:43,274 --> 02:14:45,065
Hello, beastie.

1172
02:15:33,966 --> 02:15:38,129
Jack Sparrow, our debt is settled.

1173
02:15:39,515 --> 02:15:41,721
The captain goes down with his ship.

1174
02:15:41,851 --> 02:15:45,800
Turns out not even Jack Sparrow
can best the devil.

1175
02:15:51,156 --> 02:15:53,693
Open the chest.

1176
02:15:54,869 --> 02:15:57,359
Open the chest. I need to see it!

1177
02:16:13,017 --> 02:16:18,971
Damn you, Jack Sparrow!

1178
02:16:24,825 --> 02:16:27,529
The last of our ships is returned.

1179
02:16:27,662 --> 02:16:30,034
Is there any news on the chest?

1180
02:16:30,164 --> 02:16:35,750
None. But one of the ships
did pick up a man adrift at sea.

1181
02:16:35,880 --> 02:16:38,002
He had these.

1182
02:16:45,018 --> 02:16:48,137
I took the liberty of filling in my name.

1183
02:16:55,741 --> 02:16:59,785
If you intend to claim these,
then you must have something to trade.

1184
02:16:59,912 --> 02:17:02,485
Do you have the compass?

1185
02:17:02,624 --> 02:17:04,415
Better.

1186
02:17:11,636 --> 02:17:14,126
The heart of Davy Jones.

1187
02:18:09,629 --> 02:18:12,036
Against the cold

1188
02:18:12,174 --> 02:18:14,462
and the sorrow.

1189
02:18:24,773 --> 02:18:27,310
It's a shame.

1190
02:18:27,443 --> 02:18:29,850
I know you're thinking that with the Pearl

1191
02:18:29,989 --> 02:18:35,327
you could have captured the devil
and set free your father's soul.

1192
02:18:35,454 --> 02:18:38,158
Doesn't matter now.

1193
02:18:38,291 --> 02:18:40,828
The Pearl is gone,

1194
02:18:40,961 --> 02:18:42,870
along with its captain.

1195
02:18:43,006 --> 02:18:45,164
Aye.

1196
02:18:45,300 --> 02:18:49,167
And already the world
seems a bit less bright.

1197
02:18:49,306 --> 02:18:52,390
He fooled us all right till the end,

1198
02:18:52,518 --> 02:18:56,218
but I guess that honest streak
finally won out.

1199
02:18:59,151 --> 02:19:00,858
To Jack Sparrow.

1200
02:19:00,987 --> 02:19:05,944
- Never another like Captain Jack.
- He was a gentleman of fortune, he was.

1201
02:19:06,578 --> 02:19:08,617
He was a good man.

1202
02:19:29,358 --> 02:19:33,402
If there was anything could be done
to bring him back...

1203
02:19:34,823 --> 02:19:38,487
- Elizabeth.
- Would you do it?

1204
02:19:39,789 --> 02:19:42,077
What would you...

1205
02:19:45,588 --> 02:19:49,455
What would any of you
be willing to do, hm?

1206
02:19:50,678 --> 02:19:54,545
Would you sail to the ends of the Earth
and beyond

1207
02:19:54,683 --> 02:19:59,724
to fetch back witty Jack
and him precious Pearl?

1208
02:20:05,906 --> 02:20:07,104
Aye.

1209
02:20:08,242 --> 02:20:09,522
Aye.

1210
02:20:10,204 --> 02:20:11,366
Aye.

1211
02:20:12,749 --> 02:20:13,744
Aye.

1212
02:20:16,795 --> 02:20:18,834
Yes.

1213
02:20:21,135 --> 02:20:22,333
Aye.

1214
02:20:22,469 --> 02:20:24,426
All right.

1215
02:20:25,515 --> 02:20:32,513
But if you go and brave the weird
and haunted shores at world's end,

1216
02:20:33,651 --> 02:20:39,902
then you will need a captain
who knows those waters.

1217
02:20:54,846 --> 02:20:58,973
So tell me,
what's become of my ship?

